1
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:05,958
[girls giggle]

2
00:00:06,708 --> 00:00:08,167
Here we go, here we go. Okay.

3
00:00:08,542 --> 00:00:10,166
- Just to let you know...
- Mm-hmm.

4
00:00:10,167 --> 00:00:12,291
- Your girl finna be bigger than Jobs.
- Right.

5
00:00:12,292 --> 00:00:13,499
- Bigger than Gates.
- Right.

6
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,624
- Bigger than Pym.
- Right.

7
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:15,749
- Bigger than Stark.
- Yeah.

8
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:17,583
- Combined.
- [both laugh]

9
00:00:18,375 --> 00:00:19,375
Okay, what about you?

10
00:00:19,792 --> 00:00:21,917
- What do you want more than anything?
- [Natalie] <i>Hmm...</i>

11
00:00:22,583 --> 00:00:23,582
[smacks lips]

12
00:00:23,583 --> 00:00:25,541
A night with Thor and his hammer.
[gasps]

13
00:00:25,542 --> 00:00:26,874
All right, see, now we gotta cut

14
00:00:26,875 --> 00:00:28,166
- the video off.
- [moans]

15
00:00:28,167 --> 00:00:29,332
Okay, let me start over.

16
00:00:29,333 --> 00:00:30,750
No, I just...

17
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:33,208
I just wanna be happy.

18
00:00:33,917 --> 00:00:35,124
[both laugh]

19
00:00:35,125 --> 00:00:37,207
- Happy?
- [sniffling]

20
00:00:37,208 --> 00:00:38,625
[chuckles, whimpers]

21
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:40,542
- I know.
- [laughs]

22
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:47,000
[♪ intriguing music playing]

23
00:01:01,375 --> 00:01:05,291
[Riri] <i>I'm here because I can be
the greatest inventor of my generation,</i>

24
00:01:05,292 --> 00:01:08,207
<i>which doesn't mean much
without the resources</i>

25
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:10,124
<i>to bring my ideas to life.</i>

26
00:01:10,125 --> 00:01:14,791
<i>After my internship abroad,
I realized I'mma need the equivalent</i>

27
00:01:14,792 --> 00:01:18,457
<i>of a whole vibranium mountain to be great.</i>

28
00:01:18,458 --> 00:01:20,417
<i>I need cash. Money.</i>

29
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:24,791
<i>Do you think Tony Stark would be
Tony Stark if he wasn't a billionaire?</i>

30
00:01:24,792 --> 00:01:27,917
<i>No shade. That's just the way
the world works.</i>

31
00:01:28,542 --> 00:01:32,124
<i>And the AI for my prototype
already costs millions of dollars,</i>

32
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:33,666
<i>which, clearly, I don't have.</i>

33
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:38,666
<i>So, yeah, my means of fundraising
have been a little experimental.</i>

34
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:39,832
[boy exclaims]

35
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,375
All right, dude, this is awesome.

36
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,082
I'm gonna get an A?

37
00:01:45,083 --> 00:01:48,042
You might mess around
and get a fellowship.

38
00:01:50,542 --> 00:01:52,625
<i>But without real money,
I gotta get creative.</i>

39
00:01:53,792 --> 00:01:58,916
<i>My method goes beyond rules and red tape.
I want to continue Mr. Stark's legacy</i>

40
00:01:58,917 --> 00:02:00,750
<i>and revolutionize safety.</i>

41
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,582
<i>My suits would shorten the response time
of first responders, firefighters,</i>

42
00:02:06,583 --> 00:02:08,499
<i>search and rescue teams,
et cetera.</i>

43
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:12,541
<i>Working on this level of innovation
does not come without risks.</i>

44
00:02:12,542 --> 00:02:13,792
[device powering up]

45
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:15,583
[yelps]

46
00:02:18,042 --> 00:02:19,499
- [screams]
- [Riri gasps]

47
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,124
[devices exploding]

48
00:02:21,125 --> 00:02:24,957
Ah! She did something!
Everybody out! Move!

49
00:02:24,958 --> 00:02:27,042
[Professor Wilkes grunts, sighs]

50
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:32,375
With all that considered, I thought...

51
00:02:33,375 --> 00:02:38,917
maybe you could convince the board
to extend my grant, just one more year.

52
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:41,582
If I could just get my idea
off the ground,

53
00:02:41,583 --> 00:02:43,999
help would never be too late.

54
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,125
This could change the world.

55
00:02:47,583 --> 00:02:49,500
- [sighs]
- [jug clattering]

56
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,000
[Professor Wilkes] Sorry.

57
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,457
[Dean Choi] Riri, I understand
why this project is a passion of yours,

58
00:02:56,458 --> 00:02:58,541
but you've been here over four years,

59
00:02:58,542 --> 00:03:01,332
with as many
disciplinary hearings on record,

60
00:03:01,333 --> 00:03:04,333
and still no true matriculation
towards a degree.

61
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:08,250
Due respect, Dean Choi,
what do I get out of matriculation?

62
00:03:08,958 --> 00:03:12,582
Some job in Palo Alto
with a tiny window

63
00:03:12,583 --> 00:03:14,541
and a bunch of guys
I have to pretend to like?

64
00:03:14,542 --> 00:03:16,750
[smacks lips, sighs deeply]

65
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:20,833
I wanna build something undeniable.

66
00:03:21,667 --> 00:03:24,082
I want real-life scientific epiphanies.

67
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:27,083
You want me to do homework
I have to explain to my professors.

68
00:03:28,583 --> 00:03:29,792
You want me to be small.

69
00:03:31,917 --> 00:03:32,958
But I refuse.

70
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:35,250
Riri...

71
00:03:36,208 --> 00:03:37,749
that's a hot pile of garbage.

72
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:39,375
Trash, and you know it.

73
00:03:40,083 --> 00:03:41,667
Who here asked you to be small?

74
00:03:42,917 --> 00:03:44,417
It's not a rhetorical question.

75
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,749
I brought you here
because the field would've

76
00:03:46,750 --> 00:03:48,500
devoured 15-year-old you.

77
00:03:49,042 --> 00:03:51,707
So we tailored a course of study
to your needs.

78
00:03:51,708 --> 00:03:56,166
And I work hard to get qualified people
like us into seats here.

79
00:03:56,167 --> 00:03:57,875
But you're making it impossible.

80
00:03:58,625 --> 00:04:01,874
I allowed some leniency,
maybe too much,

81
00:04:01,875 --> 00:04:04,000
given your situation, but...

82
00:04:04,833 --> 00:04:05,833
[sighs]

83
00:04:06,875 --> 00:04:11,749
You sold completed assignments to students
at every school in the area.

84
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:13,832
Cambridge, Providence, New Haven--

85
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:18,832
Quite frankly, you have been
a horrible steward of Stark's legacy.

86
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:23,000
MIT has a zero-tolerance policy
on plagiarism.

87
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:25,875
So, this ends with us.

88
00:04:27,708 --> 00:04:28,708
You're right.

89
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,582
I somberly accept responsibility

90
00:04:34,583 --> 00:04:37,167
and will get right back to my studies.

91
00:04:44,458 --> 00:04:47,207
Given your history here
and the financial impact

92
00:04:47,208 --> 00:04:50,458
of yesterday's incident, it's too late.

93
00:04:53,833 --> 00:04:57,000
- You're expelling me?
- Effective immediately.

94
00:05:00,375 --> 00:05:02,042
Is there anything you would like to say?

95
00:05:06,583 --> 00:05:08,250
[stutters, breathes shakily]

96
00:05:12,208 --> 00:05:14,500
[guard] Just gather your stuff.
We'll wait for you by the exit.

97
00:05:18,708 --> 00:05:20,708
[♪ funky music playing,
COBRAH "TEA"]

98
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:26,250
[drawer opens]

99
00:05:26,833 --> 00:05:28,832
<i>♪ Turn it on, cook it up ♪</i>

100
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:30,791
<i>♪ Make it boil, let it soak ♪</i>

101
00:05:30,792 --> 00:05:32,916
<i>♪ Is it hot? Is it cold? ♪</i>

102
00:05:32,917 --> 00:05:34,791
<i>♪ What degree? Spill the tea ♪</i>

103
00:05:34,792 --> 00:05:36,749
<i>♪ Turn it on, cook it up ♪</i>

104
00:05:36,750 --> 00:05:38,958
<i>♪ Make it boil, let it soak... ♪</i>

105
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:41,499
[Harini] Why are you guys
doing this to Riri?

106
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:44,124
We're just here to make sure
she doesn't take anything that's not hers.

107
00:05:44,125 --> 00:05:46,500
- [Riri] I would never!
- [footsteps thudding]

108
00:05:47,083 --> 00:05:48,916
[Harini] She built that
with her own grant money.

109
00:05:48,917 --> 00:05:50,125
I did that, right?

110
00:05:52,750 --> 00:05:54,208
[electronic beeping]

111
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:56,917
[all exclaiming]

112
00:05:58,625 --> 00:05:59,624
[man exclaims]

113
00:05:59,625 --> 00:06:01,083
[Riri] Whoo!

114
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,500
[chuckles]

115
00:06:04,083 --> 00:06:05,208
Yeah!

116
00:06:07,083 --> 00:06:09,082
Hey, TRVOR! You in there?

117
00:06:09,083 --> 00:06:10,207
'Sup? I'm TRVOR,

118
00:06:10,208 --> 00:06:13,582
MIT's teaching robot
and virtual operational resource,

119
00:06:13,583 --> 00:06:15,249
and your very own AI assistant.

120
00:06:15,250 --> 00:06:17,832
Please say or enter
your student ID to proceed.

121
00:06:17,833 --> 00:06:20,291
Come on, bruh.
Don't you recognize my voice?

122
00:06:20,292 --> 00:06:23,041
- 'Sup? I'm TRVOR, MIT's--
- Okay, okay.

123
00:06:23,042 --> 00:06:25,541
- 3-0-2-1-6-R-W.
- [chimes]

124
00:06:25,542 --> 00:06:28,541
Access granted. How can I help you today?

125
00:06:28,542 --> 00:06:29,749
Take us home, TRVOR.

126
00:06:29,750 --> 00:06:32,666
- [TRVOR] Home is not a known location.
- Chicago.

127
00:06:32,667 --> 00:06:35,332
- Chicago, is an American rock band--
- [distorted buzzing]

128
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:40,207
Goofy-ass AI.
Set a course to Chicago, Illinois.

129
00:06:40,208 --> 00:06:43,625
You got it, Reh-Ree.
Setting course to Chicago, Illinois.

130
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:45,249
Thank you.

131
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:47,333
[♪ "TEA" by COBRAH continues]

132
00:06:49,542 --> 00:06:51,416
<i>♪ Turn it on, cook it up ♪</i>

133
00:06:51,417 --> 00:06:53,582
<i>♪ Make it boil, let it soak ♪</i>

134
00:06:53,583 --> 00:06:55,457
<i>♪ Is it hot? Is it cold? ♪</i>

135
00:06:55,458 --> 00:06:57,458
<i>♪ What degree? Spill the tea ♪</i>

136
00:07:03,167 --> 00:07:05,000
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

137
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:10,500
Yo.

138
00:07:11,417 --> 00:07:13,042
- You guys in position or what?
- [typing]

139
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,583
[Stuart] System disabled.
Good to go, guys. Rock and roll.

140
00:07:17,208 --> 00:07:19,167
[Roz] Blood Siblings to John,
we're ready.

141
00:07:23,208 --> 00:07:24,208
[Stuart] <i>You're up, Clown.</i>

142
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:29,707
- [digital beeping]
- [system chirps]

143
00:07:29,708 --> 00:07:32,208
[groans] Shit!
The system just went back online.

144
00:07:32,542 --> 00:07:33,583
<i>Hang on a second.</i>

145
00:07:34,125 --> 00:07:36,041
Don't worry. I got it.
Don't panic. I'm fixing it.

146
00:07:36,042 --> 00:07:37,416
[breathes deeply]

147
00:07:37,417 --> 00:07:38,500
[Stuart] <i>Come on.</i>

148
00:07:39,083 --> 00:07:40,166
Don't freeze on me.

149
00:07:40,167 --> 00:07:41,499
Come on, Stu!

150
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:46,374
It's Rampage. Not Stu.
It's not Stuart. Rampage.

151
00:07:46,375 --> 00:07:47,957
[Jeri] <i>Yeah, we're not calling you that.</i>

152
00:07:47,958 --> 00:07:50,333
[Stuart] <i>Why not?
Everybody else has a codename.</i>

153
00:07:50,792 --> 00:07:51,916
Yeah, but yours is dumb.

154
00:07:51,917 --> 00:07:53,957
You know what? Piss off.

155
00:07:53,958 --> 00:07:57,417
Stuart. My guy. What is the holdup?

156
00:07:58,125 --> 00:07:59,499
- Uh...
- [digital beeping]

157
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:01,708
[exclaims] Okay. System disabled.
You're good to go again.

158
00:08:06,125 --> 00:08:07,833
I call it Dragon's Breath.

159
00:08:08,625 --> 00:08:09,749
[Roz] <i>We are in.</i>

160
00:08:09,750 --> 00:08:11,250
[Slug] All right. Let's get to work.

161
00:08:15,250 --> 00:08:16,417
Take a right.

162
00:08:16,750 --> 00:08:18,125
Third corridor on the left.

163
00:08:20,417 --> 00:08:23,625
The asset is right behind that door.

164
00:08:26,125 --> 00:08:27,708
[alarm blaring]

165
00:08:28,875 --> 00:08:30,374
Why's the alarm goin' off?

166
00:08:30,375 --> 00:08:31,792
Oh, no, no, no, no.

167
00:08:32,667 --> 00:08:33,957
I thought we were in the clear, Stu.

168
00:08:33,958 --> 00:08:35,207
Damn it! [grunts]

169
00:08:35,208 --> 00:08:38,417
Too late. Blood Sibs, y'all got company.

170
00:08:39,875 --> 00:08:41,208
[both grunting]

171
00:08:44,208 --> 00:08:46,500
[♪ dramatic music playing]

172
00:08:48,208 --> 00:08:49,208
[grunting]

173
00:08:49,667 --> 00:08:50,667
[man groans]

174
00:08:53,250 --> 00:08:55,166
- [groans]
- [both grunting]

175
00:08:55,167 --> 00:08:57,791
- [Slug] I lost visual.
- I can fix this. I can fix this.

176
00:08:57,792 --> 00:08:59,499
The alarm must've triggered
a signal block.

177
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:01,082
Shit! Yo, listen up.

178
00:09:01,083 --> 00:09:03,375
Cops are gonna be here any minute,
thanks to our boy Stuey.

179
00:09:04,083 --> 00:09:05,875
Slug's gonna text you
new pick-up coordinates now.

180
00:09:14,333 --> 00:09:15,542
Yo, Hood.

181
00:09:17,042 --> 00:09:18,167
Done strippin' the van.

182
00:09:18,958 --> 00:09:20,292
You need anything else?

183
00:09:22,375 --> 00:09:23,792
Nah, nah. You guys are good.

184
00:09:24,292 --> 00:09:25,583
Go home, get some rest.

185
00:09:27,083 --> 00:09:28,541
- What?
- [Clown] Mm-hmm.

186
00:09:28,542 --> 00:09:31,208
- [Jeri clears throat]
- Hey! It wasn't my fault!

187
00:09:31,917 --> 00:09:33,542
[John] So, what should we do about Stuart?

188
00:09:34,458 --> 00:09:35,458
[The Hood] Fire him.

189
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:38,208
Oh, but make sure you find
his replacement first.

190
00:09:38,708 --> 00:09:41,000
- Nah, Slug could do the next job.
- How?

191
00:09:42,292 --> 00:09:45,333
They're software.
We need hardware for this one.

192
00:09:46,125 --> 00:09:48,332
Where are we gonna find someone
in the next couple days?

193
00:09:48,333 --> 00:09:51,207
Hell, we still got TNNL or Heirlum,
the big job--

194
00:09:51,208 --> 00:09:52,542
[whooshing]

195
00:09:55,250 --> 00:09:57,458
- Yo, you seein' this?
- Holy hell.

196
00:09:59,625 --> 00:10:03,083
Vital signs indicate corporeal overload.
Are you all right?

197
00:10:03,667 --> 00:10:04,667
I'm fine.

198
00:10:05,250 --> 00:10:07,792
Okay, Reh-Ree.
Can I help you with anything else?

199
00:10:08,417 --> 00:10:10,666
- Yeah. Text Xavier.
- Sure.

200
00:10:10,667 --> 00:10:13,082
- Tell him to meet me at my mom's.
- Texting Xavier.

201
00:10:13,083 --> 00:10:14,583
I need an escape plan.

202
00:10:15,875 --> 00:10:18,291
Hey, TRVOR, stabilize the cabin pressure.

203
00:10:18,292 --> 00:10:20,082
- I need some more air.
- Okay, Reh-Ree.

204
00:10:20,083 --> 00:10:21,874
- [distorted] Stabilizing cabin pressure.
- No.

205
00:10:21,875 --> 00:10:24,625
No, don't give up on me now, man.
We're almost there.

206
00:10:25,875 --> 00:10:28,083
Thanos was just the beginning!

207
00:10:28,625 --> 00:10:31,749
{\an8}- Hello? TRVOR, where you at?
- [alarms chirp]

208
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:34,917
{\an8}Nah. Wait, hold on.

209
00:10:35,333 --> 00:10:36,333
{\an8}Wait, wait!

210
00:10:39,250 --> 00:10:41,416
Tony can't save us this time!

211
00:10:41,417 --> 00:10:42,583
[clangs]

212
00:10:43,375 --> 00:10:45,207
[Riri] What was that?

213
00:10:45,208 --> 00:10:46,999
Switch to manual control.

214
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,750
- [crackling]
- Ooh!

215
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,125
[screams] TRVOR!

216
00:10:53,833 --> 00:10:54,833
[grunting]

217
00:10:58,125 --> 00:10:59,875
[coughing]

218
00:11:02,167 --> 00:11:04,207
- [bicycle bell rings]
- [man] A-yo, get off the street.

219
00:11:04,208 --> 00:11:05,291
[Riri groans]

220
00:11:05,292 --> 00:11:07,583
[♪ soul jazz music playing]

221
00:11:18,458 --> 00:11:21,582
[whistles] Yo. Whoo-hoo, IronGirl.

222
00:11:21,583 --> 00:11:22,999
Say, say, say.

223
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:24,249
[man 1] Yo, check her out, man.

224
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:26,875
- Dang! That was you who crashed just now?
- [man 2] Okay.

225
00:11:27,708 --> 00:11:31,916
Do you need, like, a Band-Aid
or a ice pack or somethin'?

226
00:11:31,917 --> 00:11:35,582
[man 2] Hey, I know you hear me!
Come on, my bad. Is it, uh, is it IronMa?

227
00:11:35,583 --> 00:11:38,082
- [man 1] A-yo, IronChick!
- W-W-W-Wait.

228
00:11:38,083 --> 00:11:39,792
I can carry your things for you.

229
00:11:41,167 --> 00:11:42,542
Check it out, check it out.

230
00:11:43,042 --> 00:11:46,375
I'm not trying to be mean,
but you look a hot mess.

231
00:11:46,917 --> 00:11:49,333
And you're attracting attention.

232
00:11:52,750 --> 00:11:55,917
So, uh, what am I supposed to call you?

233
00:11:57,167 --> 00:11:58,708
You can call me Ms. Riri,

234
00:11:59,750 --> 00:12:02,750
'cause I'm grown and you're little.

235
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:05,750
What does your momma call you?

236
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,375
Riri Williams, Bad Girl Genius.

237
00:12:11,875 --> 00:12:13,750
Hey. Whoo!

238
00:12:14,333 --> 00:12:16,667
'Cause that is terrible.

239
00:12:17,583 --> 00:12:19,000
That's your introduction?

240
00:12:20,542 --> 00:12:23,875
- I'm Landon.
- [Riri] Well, nice to meet you, Landon.

241
00:12:24,375 --> 00:12:25,417
Look, we're here.

242
00:12:25,958 --> 00:12:28,958
[♪ soulful music playing,
Gorgeous George "You Need Me"]

243
00:12:34,792 --> 00:12:38,166
Twenty dollars to the door.
Five more if you want me to take it up.

244
00:12:38,167 --> 00:12:40,082
- Twenty dollars?
- Yeah.

245
00:12:40,083 --> 00:12:41,583
That's highway robbery.

246
00:12:42,083 --> 00:12:43,542
Hey, that sounds like a you problem.

247
00:12:45,792 --> 00:12:46,792
[scoffs]

248
00:12:47,917 --> 00:12:49,250
[girls giggling]

249
00:12:54,125 --> 00:12:57,541
[Madeline] A lot of kids act out
for years after a traumatic event.

250
00:12:57,542 --> 00:12:58,874
[Ronnie] Hmm.

251
00:12:58,875 --> 00:13:00,750
But Riri's always been like this though.

252
00:13:01,333 --> 00:13:03,582
And, see, I don't know how much of it
is her acting out

253
00:13:03,583 --> 00:13:06,624
and how much of it is Riri being Riri.
You know what I'm sayin'?

254
00:13:06,625 --> 00:13:09,582
[Madeline] Yeah, but I can sense
it's still weighing on Riri

255
00:13:09,583 --> 00:13:11,457
after five years.

256
00:13:11,458 --> 00:13:14,874
Not being able to say goodbye
to Natalie or her stepdad.

257
00:13:14,875 --> 00:13:16,332
Such a tragedy.

258
00:13:16,333 --> 00:13:17,749
Come bring Riri by the shop...

259
00:13:17,750 --> 00:13:19,332
- [sighs]
- ...so she can meet my daughter.

260
00:13:19,333 --> 00:13:21,416
They can get into some trouble.

261
00:13:21,417 --> 00:13:23,541
- [Ronnie] Oh. Uh-oh.
- [both laugh]

262
00:13:23,542 --> 00:13:27,042
[Madeline] If they're anything like us,
we better get some bail money ready.

263
00:13:27,167 --> 00:13:28,417
[women laughing]

264
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:30,500
Riri?

265
00:13:31,292 --> 00:13:32,292
[Riri] Yes, Ma.

266
00:13:33,583 --> 00:13:34,583
[Ronnie] When did you--

267
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,417
[parts clattering]

268
00:13:41,542 --> 00:13:42,583
[sighs]

269
00:13:43,333 --> 00:13:46,083
Baby, oh, get in here! Oh!

270
00:13:48,583 --> 00:13:50,749
Hey, Ms. Stanton.

271
00:13:50,750 --> 00:13:53,333
Give thanks, Ms. Riri.

272
00:13:53,917 --> 00:13:55,707
Come here, baby. Mmm.

273
00:13:55,708 --> 00:13:57,042
It's good to see you.

274
00:13:57,542 --> 00:13:59,250
What the hell happened to you?

275
00:14:00,250 --> 00:14:02,207
I should've known, that damned suit again.

276
00:14:02,208 --> 00:14:05,583
Why do you insist
on building your own death trap?

277
00:14:07,083 --> 00:14:08,292
Because I can.

278
00:14:08,875 --> 00:14:09,958
What is that supposed to mean?

279
00:14:11,875 --> 00:14:16,124
Just clearing the stank aura that
you drug in with you. Don't mind me.

280
00:14:16,125 --> 00:14:17,582
[women laugh]

281
00:14:17,583 --> 00:14:20,124
So y'all gonna make me
hold crystals about my feelings now?

282
00:14:20,125 --> 00:14:21,582
- [Ronnie] Mmm. Maddie?
- [Madeline] Hmm.

283
00:14:21,583 --> 00:14:25,292
Which crystal is for wise-asses
who've been kicked out of school?

284
00:14:26,333 --> 00:14:27,707
- Black obsidian.
- Ooh.

285
00:14:27,708 --> 00:14:30,375
- [woman 1] Mm-hmm. You know that's right.
- [women chuckle]

286
00:14:32,125 --> 00:14:33,125
You knew?

287
00:14:33,917 --> 00:14:37,166
I bribed a janitor there
your first week to look out for you.

288
00:14:37,167 --> 00:14:39,832
We keep in touch. See, he called.

289
00:14:39,833 --> 00:14:42,457
You know you can always do the same?

290
00:14:42,458 --> 00:14:44,291
[Madeline] You know, Riri,

291
00:14:44,292 --> 00:14:47,958
black obsidian is also really good
to have in times of grief.

292
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:50,207
Nobody is grieving.

293
00:14:50,208 --> 00:14:51,833
Ms. Stanton, I'm fine.

294
00:14:52,917 --> 00:14:55,291
Please tell me to go to my room
and think about what I've done.

295
00:14:55,292 --> 00:14:57,707
- No. We have guests. Do not embarrass me.
- Come on. [smacks lips]

296
00:14:57,708 --> 00:14:59,499
[Ronnie] Just sit down
and have a cup of tea...

297
00:14:59,500 --> 00:15:00,874
Hey, Black people.

298
00:15:00,875 --> 00:15:01,958
[Madeline] Uh, hello.

299
00:15:03,750 --> 00:15:05,458
Hey, so I know you just got back.

300
00:15:06,542 --> 00:15:08,167
- You free tomorrow?
- Tomorrow?

301
00:15:09,875 --> 00:15:11,292
Nah, how about right now?

302
00:15:11,875 --> 00:15:13,458
Yeah, now is cool.

303
00:15:14,083 --> 00:15:15,583
[Ronnie] Whoa, whoa. Wait, wait.

304
00:15:16,125 --> 00:15:18,125
- We'll be back late.
- [Ronnie] You can't just leave the--

305
00:15:19,042 --> 00:15:21,125
[Xavier] I mean,
it's Friday night, almost eight.

306
00:15:22,917 --> 00:15:23,917
So...

307
00:15:24,708 --> 00:15:26,875
So, a hot girl's
supposed to be at the clubs.

308
00:15:27,542 --> 00:15:29,125
Trying to get some drinks free.

309
00:15:30,375 --> 00:15:31,375
Hmm.

310
00:15:32,208 --> 00:15:36,542
But you, Riri Williams,
are making an iron suit.

311
00:15:37,042 --> 00:15:39,458
People are obsessed with them.

312
00:15:41,333 --> 00:15:43,083
I could build a thousand other things...

313
00:15:43,875 --> 00:15:44,875
and no one would care.

314
00:15:46,208 --> 00:15:47,792
But if I could build one of them?

315
00:15:48,875 --> 00:15:50,792
Better than everyone else?

316
00:15:51,792 --> 00:15:53,792
People got no choice but to respect me.

317
00:15:54,583 --> 00:15:56,708
Smarter AI, stronger armor.

318
00:16:00,875 --> 00:16:03,041
Does that mean you're gonna
stick around Chicago to build it?

319
00:16:03,042 --> 00:16:05,333
Ooh. I need a job that pays,

320
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:10,833
and a lab so massive they don't notice
if I finesse a resource here or there.

321
00:16:12,625 --> 00:16:14,833
Thinking Dubai...

322
00:16:16,375 --> 00:16:19,250
[huffs] All the way around the world
to prove a point.

323
00:16:20,208 --> 00:16:21,875
Everything here feels so...

324
00:16:23,667 --> 00:16:24,917
I don't know, small.

325
00:16:32,542 --> 00:16:33,708
[Xavier] I hope you don't mind.

326
00:16:34,208 --> 00:16:35,999
Just wanted to show you
the mural they put up

327
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,625
with your stepdad and my sister
while you were away.

328
00:16:39,333 --> 00:16:40,625
Not bad, right?

329
00:16:44,875 --> 00:16:45,874
I miss 'em both.

330
00:16:45,875 --> 00:16:47,332
- [Gary] <i>Riri?</i>
- [Riri laughs]

331
00:16:47,333 --> 00:16:49,749
- [Natalie] <i>I just wanna be happy.</i>
- [Riri laughs]

332
00:16:49,750 --> 00:16:52,792
[Xavier] You remember that one time
your stepdad got stuck under the Celica,

333
00:16:53,458 --> 00:16:56,249
like, the jack gave out?
He spent hours down there...

334
00:16:56,250 --> 00:16:57,458
[chuckles] refused to admit it.

335
00:16:58,958 --> 00:17:00,499
[laughs]

336
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:03,041
He used to say, "The car needed
a glass of alkaline water

337
00:17:03,042 --> 00:17:04,374
'cause it was thirsty."

338
00:17:04,375 --> 00:17:05,542
[chuckles softly]

339
00:17:08,083 --> 00:17:10,707
He used to sing along
to every song on the radio.

340
00:17:10,708 --> 00:17:12,416
[Gary] <i>♪ On the other side
Of the rainbow... ♪</i>

341
00:17:12,417 --> 00:17:14,124
[Xavier] <i>Word for word, note for note.</i>

342
00:17:14,125 --> 00:17:17,124
And you and him used to work
every waking moment

343
00:17:17,125 --> 00:17:21,458
on that trash Barracuda.
Whatever happened to it?

344
00:17:24,875 --> 00:17:25,875
I don't have it.

345
00:17:26,875 --> 00:17:28,083
I don't have it anymore.

346
00:17:29,167 --> 00:17:32,292
I sold it for money for my suit.

347
00:17:36,125 --> 00:17:37,833
Sorry I wasn't there that night...

348
00:17:39,708 --> 00:17:41,082
when everything happened.

349
00:17:41,083 --> 00:17:43,167
[♪ somber music playing]

350
00:17:50,333 --> 00:17:51,417
I'll get you home.

351
00:17:57,083 --> 00:17:58,667
I made you something. Well...

352
00:17:59,458 --> 00:18:03,082
I made me something.
A mixtape that I thought you might like,

353
00:18:03,083 --> 00:18:05,375
so I made you a copy.

354
00:18:06,250 --> 00:18:09,083
Uh, and you'll need those, too.

355
00:18:10,375 --> 00:18:14,083
Here. Listen to it later.

356
00:18:15,333 --> 00:18:16,707
I got you.

357
00:18:16,708 --> 00:18:18,958
See you tomorrow.

358
00:18:20,667 --> 00:18:22,667
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

359
00:18:45,083 --> 00:18:47,083
Does it have to be
that particular converter?

360
00:18:47,750 --> 00:18:51,167
I mean, I could say no, but yeah.

361
00:18:52,125 --> 00:18:53,292
It's gotta be that one.

362
00:18:54,208 --> 00:18:57,958
This will help build the hardware,
but I still gotta figure out my AI...

363
00:18:59,125 --> 00:19:00,167
So it's a start.

364
00:19:01,208 --> 00:19:03,792
Well, then go tell Jim,
and I'll load everything else.

365
00:19:04,708 --> 00:19:05,708
All right.

366
00:19:06,542 --> 00:19:08,624
[telephone rings]

367
00:19:08,625 --> 00:19:11,124
[man] Yeah, all right, hang on.

368
00:19:11,125 --> 00:19:15,041
Hey, Jim, I got Tony on line one.
He's asking for another extension.

369
00:19:15,042 --> 00:19:18,541
Hey. I told you
this guy need to pay in full!

370
00:19:18,542 --> 00:19:20,625
- [grunts]
- [man] All right. All right. You got it.

371
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:23,750
Hey, listen. Are you there?
You're talking like--

372
00:19:25,792 --> 00:19:28,417
Jim... [sighs] I just gotta tell you
straight off, okay?

373
00:19:28,917 --> 00:19:30,958
Riri Williams! [chuckles]

374
00:19:31,708 --> 00:19:33,666
Would you look at that!

375
00:19:33,667 --> 00:19:39,916
I was just telling Iris
about your big, uh, Tony Stark fellowship

376
00:19:39,917 --> 00:19:41,582
at that MIT school.

377
00:19:41,583 --> 00:19:44,332
Yeah, yeah, that was such a blessing.

378
00:19:44,333 --> 00:19:47,791
When you were a little girl,
um, nine, ten,

379
00:19:47,792 --> 00:19:51,042
you used to come in here
and haggle with me. [chuckles]

380
00:19:51,708 --> 00:19:56,166
You always were with, uh, that
best friend of yours, uh...

381
00:19:56,167 --> 00:19:57,375
What was her name?

382
00:19:58,250 --> 00:20:00,875
- [stutters] Natalie.
- Yeah. [chuckling] Natalie.

383
00:20:01,667 --> 00:20:04,750
And I knew it.
I knew you'd be big time

384
00:20:05,333 --> 00:20:08,582
and come back here
and spend all your money with me.

385
00:20:08,583 --> 00:20:09,667
[Riri] Jim, actually...

386
00:20:10,667 --> 00:20:12,791
This time, I was hopin', just this once,

387
00:20:12,792 --> 00:20:14,250
that you'd actually let me...

388
00:20:14,750 --> 00:20:17,625
Hoping you'd let her pay with large bills?

389
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:19,499
All right. Oh.

390
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:20,667
[John] You know how it is.

391
00:20:21,250 --> 00:20:23,542
Must get hella embarrassing
flauntin' what you have, right?

392
00:20:24,333 --> 00:20:25,333
Here.

393
00:20:27,750 --> 00:20:29,833
[John] You gonna
grab your change, Ms. Williams?

394
00:20:30,875 --> 00:20:33,957
This one right here, couple years ago,

395
00:20:33,958 --> 00:20:37,458
could not open the local section
of the <i>Trib</i> and not see a story on her.

396
00:20:37,583 --> 00:20:40,542
I mean, finished Ninth grade at age 11...

397
00:20:41,292 --> 00:20:46,207
started college at 15,
and here she is, in your shop,

398
00:20:46,208 --> 00:20:48,458
all grown up. Wow. [chuckles]

399
00:20:54,708 --> 00:20:55,750
[John clears throat]

400
00:20:57,750 --> 00:20:58,916
You know I had it under control.

401
00:20:58,917 --> 00:21:00,000
I know you did.

402
00:21:01,125 --> 00:21:02,125
Thanks or whatever.

403
00:21:02,625 --> 00:21:03,625
Yeah, no doubt.

404
00:21:05,042 --> 00:21:07,582
You know you're too special
to be haggling over trash, right?

405
00:21:07,583 --> 00:21:08,792
I'm serious.

406
00:21:11,167 --> 00:21:13,416
How come you're not working
at like, I don't know,

407
00:21:13,417 --> 00:21:15,000
Stark Industries or something?

408
00:21:15,458 --> 00:21:17,458
I mean, you're at least as smart
as that Tony guy, right?

409
00:21:19,125 --> 00:21:23,749
I just met you.
And he contributed immensely to the field,

410
00:21:23,750 --> 00:21:25,833
so I won't roast Tony Stark off rip.

411
00:21:27,042 --> 00:21:28,083
But...

412
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,833
I don't have a billion,
and I can't compete without resources.

413
00:21:33,417 --> 00:21:34,417
I get that.

414
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,375
You know, traditional companies,
they didn't work for me, either.

415
00:21:38,542 --> 00:21:40,417
Not for freethinkers like me and you.

416
00:21:41,125 --> 00:21:42,249
[John clears throat]

417
00:21:42,250 --> 00:21:43,792
That's why I work for someone...

418
00:21:45,208 --> 00:21:46,208
better.

419
00:21:46,792 --> 00:21:48,832
Come by tomorrow to meet him.
When you get there,

420
00:21:48,833 --> 00:21:51,250
use the elevator in the kitchen
up to the second floor.

421
00:21:52,875 --> 00:21:53,875
No pressure.

422
00:21:58,125 --> 00:21:59,124
Morning? Evening?

423
00:21:59,125 --> 00:22:01,375
Boss prefers as quickly as possible, so...

424
00:22:02,042 --> 00:22:03,082
bright and early.

425
00:22:03,083 --> 00:22:05,416
Pleasure to meet you, Ms. Williams.

426
00:22:05,417 --> 00:22:07,500
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

427
00:22:29,250 --> 00:22:30,375
The hell?

428
00:23:37,875 --> 00:23:39,250
[elevator creaks, clangs]

429
00:23:43,167 --> 00:23:44,167
[Riri] <i>Really?</i>

430
00:23:45,792 --> 00:23:46,792
Hello?

431
00:23:49,500 --> 00:23:51,458
Somebody come open this door.

432
00:23:57,625 --> 00:23:59,000
[timer beeping]

433
00:24:04,958 --> 00:24:05,958
[gasps]

434
00:24:09,333 --> 00:24:10,999
[The Hood, over speakers] <i>Welcome, Riri.</i>

435
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:12,666
<i>In less than three minutes,</i>

436
00:24:12,667 --> 00:24:15,374
<i>the air in this elevator
will no longer be breathable.</i>

437
00:24:15,375 --> 00:24:17,041
[stutters] What is this?

438
00:24:17,042 --> 00:24:18,124
[The Hood] <i>Your interview.</i>

439
00:24:18,125 --> 00:24:19,916
<i>You will suffocate and die unless--</i>

440
00:24:19,917 --> 00:24:22,832
Uh-uh, hello! This is illegal!

441
00:24:22,833 --> 00:24:25,041
[The Hood] <i>You break into the device
on the floor,</i>

442
00:24:25,042 --> 00:24:26,583
<i>where you'll find a gas mask.</i>

443
00:24:27,042 --> 00:24:28,042
<i>Good luck.</i>

444
00:24:35,042 --> 00:24:36,749
- Duct tape.
- Totally.

445
00:24:36,750 --> 00:24:39,124
I get weekend warrior vibes from this one.

446
00:24:39,125 --> 00:24:40,374
Takes one to know one.

447
00:24:40,375 --> 00:24:42,207
She's gonna use the caliper. Watch.

448
00:24:42,208 --> 00:24:45,082
It doesn't matter what she uses.
Look at her. She's shook.

449
00:24:45,083 --> 00:24:47,916
- Sweats almost as much as you, Stu.
- [all chuckling]

450
00:24:47,917 --> 00:24:49,749
- It's Rampage.
- No.

451
00:24:49,750 --> 00:24:50,832
- Sweetie, no.
- Nah, dawg.

452
00:24:50,833 --> 00:24:52,083
- Mm-mm, sorry.
- What do you mean?

453
00:24:56,292 --> 00:24:57,417
[Riri] Screw this.

454
00:24:58,542 --> 00:25:00,457
- [grunts]
- [all exclaiming]

455
00:25:00,458 --> 00:25:01,999
Pow. Pow, pow, pow!

456
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:03,667
[♪ dramatic music playing]

457
00:25:19,792 --> 00:25:20,874
[machinery whirs]

458
00:25:20,875 --> 00:25:22,332
[elevator powers back on]

459
00:25:22,333 --> 00:25:23,417
[elevator clangs]

460
00:25:25,042 --> 00:25:26,374
[Clown exclaims]

461
00:25:26,375 --> 00:25:27,749
- What?
- [John] Looks like

462
00:25:27,750 --> 00:25:29,500
- we found your replacement, Stu.
- [Roz] Okay.

463
00:25:32,125 --> 00:25:33,167
[grunts]

464
00:25:33,792 --> 00:25:35,000
- [all exclaim]
- Oh, shit!

465
00:25:35,583 --> 00:25:37,207
[Slug] Oh, my God. That's kinda clever.

466
00:25:37,208 --> 00:25:39,125
[metal creaking]

467
00:25:43,333 --> 00:25:45,458
- [grunts]
- [elevator door creaking]

468
00:25:47,958 --> 00:25:48,999
[straining]

469
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,457
- That's exactly what I would've done.
- Uh-huh.

470
00:25:51,458 --> 00:25:52,917
[The Hood clapping]

471
00:25:55,458 --> 00:25:57,500
I heard Riri Williams was a genius,

472
00:25:58,083 --> 00:25:59,416
but I said to myself,

473
00:25:59,417 --> 00:26:02,125
"Parker, you gotta meet
this kid up close."

474
00:26:02,833 --> 00:26:04,457
What makes you think you can trap me--

475
00:26:04,458 --> 00:26:06,583
Please. It wasn't personal.

476
00:26:08,333 --> 00:26:09,499
Why would you do that?

477
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:11,332
Does it matter why I did what I did?

478
00:26:11,333 --> 00:26:14,083
What matters... is what you did.

479
00:26:14,833 --> 00:26:16,166
You beat Stuart's time.

480
00:26:16,167 --> 00:26:17,249
Who is Stuart?

481
00:26:17,250 --> 00:26:18,333
Present.

482
00:26:19,917 --> 00:26:20,917
Stuart...

483
00:26:21,542 --> 00:26:22,542
Clarke?

484
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,624
Like the AURA-X fellow?
At-Rampage on the tech-makers' Discord?

485
00:26:27,625 --> 00:26:28,875
<i>Enchanté.</i>

486
00:26:29,375 --> 00:26:30,416
- Nah. No.
- Nah, dawg.

487
00:26:30,417 --> 00:26:31,625
- No.
- What'd I tell you?

488
00:26:33,375 --> 00:26:34,375
[Riri] Where am I?

489
00:26:36,375 --> 00:26:37,916
You know what? I don't even care.

490
00:26:37,917 --> 00:26:39,000
Riri, I see you.

491
