1643
01:33:19,490 --> 01:33:22,280
"a homegrown Chicago player
who became an industry titan

1644
01:33:22,281 --> 01:33:25,030
"by applying cutting edge
farming technology

1645
01:33:25,031 --> 01:33:27,615
"to specially preserved antique seeds."

1646
01:33:28,406 --> 01:33:31,072
Old meets new... on steroids.

1647
01:33:31,073 --> 01:33:33,155
They're driving local farmers
out of business.

1648
01:33:33,156 --> 01:33:35,905
[Slug] We're still running recon,
but what we do know

1649
01:33:35,906 --> 01:33:38,864
is that the entire facility
is outfitted with security

1650
01:33:38,865 --> 01:33:40,989
designed to detect any metals

1651
01:33:40,990 --> 01:33:44,030
that could be considered
a threat to the biology,

1652
01:33:44,031 --> 01:33:45,989
which means no knives,

1653
01:33:45,990 --> 01:33:49,280
no suit, and no bombs.

1654
01:33:49,281 --> 01:33:50,822
Any questions?

1655
01:33:50,823 --> 01:33:52,114
I have one.

1656
01:33:52,115 --> 01:33:55,365
Why does a bunch of good-looking people
look so pitiful?

1657
01:33:55,865 --> 01:33:59,655
Probably 'cause ya boy
slammed my head into a table

1658
01:33:59,656 --> 01:34:01,365
and still hasn't apologized yet.

1659
01:34:06,031 --> 01:34:07,448
[smacks lips] I'm sorry.

1660
01:34:08,031 --> 01:34:09,448
[Parker] Look, we all just got paid.

1661
01:34:10,115 --> 01:34:12,655
Everybody go out, have fun,
spend frivolously,

1662
01:34:12,656 --> 01:34:16,864
and when we come back with cooler heads,
we bring the heat to another rich asshole.

1663
01:34:16,865 --> 01:34:18,281
All right?

1664
01:34:26,531 --> 01:34:27,823
[Riri] Hey, Hood.

1665
01:34:29,365 --> 01:34:31,073
Can I talk to you for a second?

1666
01:34:32,323 --> 01:34:33,906
About these heists,

1667
01:34:34,615 --> 01:34:36,947
we <i>Ocean's Eleven</i> or <i>The Sopranos?</i>

1668
01:34:36,948 --> 01:34:38,031
[chuckles]

1669
01:34:38,865 --> 01:34:40,115
What's the difference?

1670
01:34:42,865 --> 01:34:44,905
I'm only doing this because I have to,

1671
01:34:44,906 --> 01:34:46,822
not because I'm a bad person.

1672
01:34:46,823 --> 01:34:49,322
You're joking, right? Everyone
you're talking about is a criminal.

1673
01:34:49,323 --> 01:34:51,490
- Including you.
- No, I'm not.

1674
01:34:52,448 --> 01:34:54,405
I'm an engineer. What I'm building is--

1675
01:34:54,406 --> 01:34:56,530
- Important?
- [train passing]

1676
01:34:56,531 --> 01:34:57,615
Iconic.

1677
01:35:00,656 --> 01:35:05,156
Look, anyone who's ever accomplished
anything iconic in life

1678
01:35:05,781 --> 01:35:08,656
has had to do some questionable things
to get it done.

1679
01:35:10,115 --> 01:35:11,615
Before my hood,

1680
01:35:13,365 --> 01:35:14,531
I was a nobody.

1681
01:35:15,573 --> 01:35:16,656
But with it,

1682
01:35:17,490 --> 01:35:19,365
maybe a little unsavory, but...

1683
01:35:21,490 --> 01:35:22,990
You know, I'm unstoppable.

1684
01:35:23,990 --> 01:35:26,281
How does that work actually?

1685
01:35:26,865 --> 01:35:28,656
I've seen something like that before.

1686
01:35:29,490 --> 01:35:30,573
Vibranium?

1687
01:35:31,948 --> 01:35:32,948
Pym tech?

1688
01:35:35,656 --> 01:35:38,280
- Magic.
- "Magic" is code for "I don't know."

1689
01:35:38,281 --> 01:35:39,697
I don't care how it works.

1690
01:35:39,698 --> 01:35:42,198
It helps me protect
who I love and what I want.

1691
01:35:43,073 --> 01:35:44,323
And that's enough for me.

1692
01:35:44,948 --> 01:35:47,073
I suspect that's enough for you too, Riri.

1693
01:35:48,031 --> 01:35:49,323
And if that makes you bad...

1694
01:35:50,948 --> 01:35:52,031
who cares?

1695
01:35:53,656 --> 01:35:55,240
But you're the genius, right?

1696
01:35:56,781 --> 01:35:59,865
And, uh... And I may be full of shit.

1697
01:36:01,031 --> 01:36:04,698
So, yeah, why don't you take
your hard-earned cash and, uh...

1698
01:36:05,448 --> 01:36:06,531
and do good?

1699
01:36:08,198 --> 01:36:09,198
Or whatever.

1700
01:36:13,031 --> 01:36:14,031
[Riri] So...

1701
01:36:15,365 --> 01:36:16,573
I got somethin' for you.

1702
01:36:20,323 --> 01:36:21,405
[grunts]

1703
01:36:21,406 --> 01:36:24,615
Now you can retire them worn-out earphones
you've been wearing.

1704
01:36:25,406 --> 01:36:26,406
[chuckles]

1705
01:36:29,031 --> 01:36:31,573
- You don't like 'em.
- [Xavier] No, they... they're great. Uh...

1706
01:36:41,240 --> 01:36:43,405
So you were dumpster divin'
a few weeks ago,

1707
01:36:43,406 --> 01:36:45,530
now you're buyin' expensive headphones?

1708
01:36:45,531 --> 01:36:47,072
That was before this job.

1709
01:36:47,073 --> 01:36:50,114
Right, that secret startup
you super conveniently can't discuss.

1710
01:36:50,115 --> 01:36:53,655
Look, if you don't want them,
that's all you had to say.

1711
01:36:53,656 --> 01:36:55,823
[Xavier] I'm not sayin' that.
They're special.

1712
01:36:56,448 --> 01:36:57,822
They're from you.

1713
01:36:57,823 --> 01:36:59,031
These?

1714
01:37:02,031 --> 01:37:03,031
These are Natalie.

1715
01:37:03,948 --> 01:37:07,656
Every scratch and scuff on these
worn-out earmuffs, Natalie put there.

1716
01:37:08,198 --> 01:37:11,240
She was always borrowin' 'em
and just disrespectful.

1717
01:37:13,656 --> 01:37:14,656
That's nice,

1718
01:37:15,365 --> 01:37:18,406
but how can you work
without falling apart?

1719
01:37:18,990 --> 01:37:21,740
Every single dent
is a memory I can hold on to.

1720
01:37:23,865 --> 01:37:26,031
I swear, when I put these on, I just...

1721
01:37:27,531 --> 01:37:29,073
I feel her soul in these.

1722
01:37:30,323 --> 01:37:31,948
She seeps into the music.

1723
01:37:33,531 --> 01:37:34,740
Makes it better.

1724
01:37:37,406 --> 01:37:38,990
Get you in your feels, huh?

1725
01:37:43,906 --> 01:37:44,906
Oh, come on, girl.

1726
01:37:46,115 --> 01:37:49,030
And I mean that completely colloquially,
not at all pejoratively.

1727
01:37:49,031 --> 01:37:50,115
[Riri] Mm.

1728
01:37:51,115 --> 01:37:52,615
From the same streets.

1729
01:37:54,323 --> 01:37:56,031
What you out here doin'?

1730
01:37:59,406 --> 01:38:02,197
- Nothing.
- You sure you're not hiding something?

1731
01:38:02,198 --> 01:38:04,365
[♪ pensive music playing]

1732
01:38:09,781 --> 01:38:11,198
[♪ music playing inside]

1733
01:38:13,240 --> 01:38:14,615
[lock opening]

1734
01:38:15,615 --> 01:38:16,740
Oh, hi.

1735
01:38:18,865 --> 01:38:20,072
- Can I help you?
- Yes.

1736
01:38:20,073 --> 01:38:23,031
Good evening, ma'am.
Is this the home of Riri Williams?

1737
01:38:24,073 --> 01:38:28,073
Daughter, why is there a sweaty,
middle-aged white man at my door?

1738
01:38:28,948 --> 01:38:31,990
Um, I'm 36, respectfully.

1739
01:38:32,573 --> 01:38:33,573
[Xavier] Hmm.

1740
01:38:35,615 --> 01:38:36,740
Joe!

1741
01:38:37,823 --> 01:38:39,655
How do you two know each other?

1742
01:38:39,656 --> 01:38:42,322
Well, Riri and I, um--

1743
01:38:42,323 --> 01:38:43,822
Met at work.

1744
01:38:43,823 --> 01:38:46,072
- Uh-huh.
- We're coworkers.

1745
01:38:46,073 --> 01:38:48,739
Coworkers, right, yeah.
We work in the same cohort...

1746
01:38:48,740 --> 01:38:50,155
uh, pod team... group.

1747
01:38:50,156 --> 01:38:51,239
[Riri] Mm-hmm.

1748
01:38:51,240 --> 01:38:53,573
I thought your work was confidential.

1749
01:38:54,156 --> 01:38:56,073
Should you really
be walking around with, uh,

1750
01:38:56,990 --> 01:38:57,989
blueprints? [chuckles]

1751
01:38:57,990 --> 01:39:00,406
My thoughts exactly.

1752
01:39:00,948 --> 01:39:02,655
How about we take this outside?

1753
01:39:02,656 --> 01:39:04,572
- We'll just be a second.
- Lovely home, Ms. Williams.

1754
01:39:04,573 --> 01:39:06,364
- Thank you.
- I'll be right back.

1755
01:39:06,365 --> 01:39:08,155
Do I really look middle-aged?

1756
01:39:08,156 --> 01:39:09,281
It's the khakis.

1757
01:39:10,323 --> 01:39:13,197
- How did you find where I live?
- Google.

1758
01:39:13,198 --> 01:39:14,989
- And why are you here?
- Right.

1759
01:39:14,990 --> 01:39:19,697
So I couldn't stop thinking about what it
felt like to hold those tools in my hand.

1760
01:39:19,698 --> 01:39:23,405
And since then I've had...
I've had so many ideas.

1761
01:39:23,406 --> 01:39:24,780
- [door closes]
- 3D-printed corneas.

1762
01:39:24,781 --> 01:39:28,155
A second iron stomach to make
food poisoning a thing of the past.

1763
01:39:28,156 --> 01:39:31,405
Nanotech muscle fibers that
increase strength and invulnerability,

1764
01:39:31,406 --> 01:39:33,697
a bio-mesh material for skin.

1765
01:39:33,698 --> 01:39:37,030
Uh, laser-enhanced fingernails
that never break or crack--

1766
01:39:37,031 --> 01:39:41,447
Listen, hey, Joe, I get that
you're having a whole moment,

1767
01:39:41,448 --> 01:39:45,073
but today is not really a good day for me,
so raincheck?

1768
01:39:46,698 --> 01:39:48,531
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

1769
01:39:51,115 --> 01:39:52,405
- No.
- Excuse me?

1770
01:39:52,406 --> 01:39:54,031
I said no.

1771
01:39:54,656 --> 01:39:56,614
You're gonna help me as much as I need,

1772
01:39:56,615 --> 01:39:59,531
or I'm gonna tell your mom
how we really know each other.

1773
01:40:06,656 --> 01:40:07,823
Give me the blueprints.

1774
01:40:08,406 --> 01:40:11,572
So take a gander at these,
and I will see you at my place tomorrow.

1775
01:40:11,573 --> 01:40:13,572
Is 6:00 good? I'm sorry, what am I saying?

1776
01:40:13,573 --> 01:40:14,698
6:00 is good.

1777
01:40:17,865 --> 01:40:18,865
See you then.

1778
01:40:19,781 --> 01:40:22,281
Thanks for teaching me
to stand up for myself!

1779
01:40:28,240 --> 01:40:29,865
Yeah, I know, I'm a criminal...

1780
01:40:30,865 --> 01:40:33,865
who's in it to optimize her suit,
so let's frickin' optimize.

1781
01:40:34,781 --> 01:40:36,405
N.A.T.A.L.I.E., run diagnostics.

1782
01:40:36,406 --> 01:40:38,198
Prep the suit for upgrade.

1783
01:40:40,406 --> 01:40:41,655
- [Natalie] Boo!
- [grunts]

1784
01:40:41,656 --> 01:40:44,822
[Natalie] I'm not upgradin' shit
until you pay me some attention.

1785
01:40:44,823 --> 01:40:46,656
What are you doing, Nat?

1786
01:40:47,281 --> 01:40:49,197
Get out!
I gotta optimize the suit before--

1787
01:40:49,198 --> 01:40:50,864
Before what?

1788
01:40:50,865 --> 01:40:53,030
Before Parker's next job?

1789
01:40:53,031 --> 01:40:54,739
You know nothin' about this man!

1790
01:40:54,740 --> 01:40:56,405
Don't clap at me!

1791
01:40:56,406 --> 01:40:58,947
Dude probably got you heistin' parts
for the next...

1792
01:40:58,948 --> 01:41:00,739
- Death Star--
- [whispers] Keep your voice down!

1793
01:41:00,740 --> 01:41:03,447
And you don't care
'cause you only seein' money.

1794
01:41:03,448 --> 01:41:04,822
You're going to get caught

1795
01:41:04,823 --> 01:41:08,655
being a high-tech criminal asset
to a halfway crook...

1796
01:41:08,656 --> 01:41:10,239
- Nat!
- You understand that, right?

1797
01:41:10,240 --> 01:41:11,989
What do I gotta do
to get you to stop talking?

1798
01:41:11,990 --> 01:41:13,822
Oh, I don't know, Ri. I have no idea.

1799
01:41:13,823 --> 01:41:15,364
Oh! Ding!

1800
01:41:15,365 --> 01:41:18,780
How about you try and treat me
like a friend and partner

1801
01:41:18,781 --> 01:41:21,365
instead of a hi-tech genie?

1802
01:41:22,781 --> 01:41:24,448
And how am I supposed to do that?

1803
01:41:25,865 --> 01:41:27,364
Let's go somewhere!

1804
01:41:27,365 --> 01:41:29,073
Hang like we used to!

1805
01:41:29,698 --> 01:41:30,698
What?

1806
01:41:31,490 --> 01:41:34,489
We didn't "used to" anything!

1807
01:41:34,490 --> 01:41:35,573
You're code.

1808
01:41:36,198 --> 01:41:38,114
That is not an efficient use of my time.

1809
01:41:38,115 --> 01:41:41,489
You know what else
isn't an efficient use of your time, Ri?

1810
01:41:41,490 --> 01:41:45,365
Tryin' to figure out
how to undo my suit override.

1811
01:41:45,948 --> 01:41:48,573
Sounds like you've got
a rock-and-a-hard-place situation.

1812
01:41:49,073 --> 01:41:50,072
Yikes.

1813
01:41:50,073 --> 01:41:51,448
This some bull!

1814
01:41:52,073 --> 01:41:54,614
How'd I get blackmailed twice
in five minutes?

1815
01:41:54,615 --> 01:41:55,823
[suit whirs]

1816
01:41:56,656 --> 01:41:57,865
[Natalie] Karma.

1817
01:41:59,073 --> 01:42:00,198
[chair creaks]

1818
01:42:01,615 --> 01:42:05,572
Fine. Just shut up about it,
we'll go first thing in the morning.

1819
01:42:05,573 --> 01:42:08,030
[Natalie] Yes! [squeals]

1820
01:42:08,031 --> 01:42:10,655
Bonnie and Clyde back on the streets!

1821
01:42:10,656 --> 01:42:13,072
Come on, best friend!

1822
01:42:13,073 --> 01:42:14,530
<i>♪ Go, best friend ♪</i>

1823
01:42:14,531 --> 01:42:16,447
- <i>♪ That's my best friend ♪</i>
- [♪ hip-hop music plays]

1824
01:42:16,448 --> 01:42:18,364
<i>♪ Go, best friend
That's my best-- ♪</i>

1825
01:42:18,365 --> 01:42:19,447
Come on.

1826
01:42:19,448 --> 01:42:20,530
<i>♪ Go, best friend ♪</i>

1827
01:42:20,531 --> 01:42:22,280
<i>♪ That's my best friend
Hey! ♪</i>

1828
01:42:22,281 --> 01:42:24,780
- [Riri] Now will you upgrade the suit?
- I've got you.

1829
01:42:24,781 --> 01:42:25,864
[♪ Saweetie "Best Friend"]

1830
01:42:25,865 --> 01:42:27,489
<i>♪ That my best friend,
she a real bad... ♪</i>

1831
01:42:27,490 --> 01:42:29,905
<i>♪ Got her own money,
she don't need no... ♪</i>

1832
01:42:29,906 --> 01:42:32,572
<i>♪ On the dance floor,
she had two, three drinks ♪</i>

1833
01:42:32,573 --> 01:42:34,405
<i>♪ Now she twerkin',
she throw it out... ♪</i>

1834
01:42:34,406 --> 01:42:37,197
Okay, test one, test two?

1835
01:42:37,198 --> 01:42:38,865
[high-pitched whirring]

1836
01:42:39,823 --> 01:42:43,780
Aw, snap. AI got a window seat.

1837
01:42:43,781 --> 01:42:46,239
Now you can project
without needin' my suit.

1838
01:42:46,240 --> 01:42:47,780
[Natalie] Hmm.

1839
01:42:47,781 --> 01:42:50,364
Now do you wanna tell me
where we're goin' or what?

1840
01:42:50,365 --> 01:42:52,697
<i>♪ That my best friend,
she a real bad... ♪</i>

1841
01:42:52,698 --> 01:42:54,072
<i>♪ Got her own money ♪</i>

1842
01:42:54,073 --> 01:42:55,489
<i>♪ She don't need no... ♪</i>

1843
01:42:55,490 --> 01:42:57,822
<i>♪ On the dance floor,
she had two, three drinks ♪</i>

1844
01:42:57,823 --> 01:43:00,239
<i>♪ Now she twerkin',
she throw it out and come back in ♪</i>

1845
01:43:00,240 --> 01:43:02,905
<i>♪ That's my best friend,
she a real bad... ♪</i>

1846
01:43:02,906 --> 01:43:05,155
<i>♪ Drive her own car,
she don't need no... ♪</i>

1847
01:43:05,156 --> 01:43:07,115
[Riri] Ooh, Cessna 152...

1848
01:43:08,656 --> 01:43:11,240
and Lycoming O-235 engine.

1849
01:43:12,365 --> 01:43:14,281
Go ahead, tell me I'm right.

1850
01:43:14,823 --> 01:43:16,614
Uhh! Yeah!

1851
01:43:16,615 --> 01:43:18,655
- Ooh. Damn, that's cold!
- Yeah.

1852
01:43:18,656 --> 01:43:20,739
- Since you wanna show off.
- Damn!

1853
01:43:20,740 --> 01:43:22,030
[cell phone vibrating]

1854
01:43:22,031 --> 01:43:24,531
Oh, peach ring down.
[exhales sharply]

1855
01:43:25,198 --> 01:43:26,198
[groans]

1856
01:43:27,198 --> 01:43:28,948
First rule of blackmail, sir.

1857
01:43:29,573 --> 01:43:31,030
Stick to your terms.

1858
01:43:31,031 --> 01:43:33,530
- What, that's Joe again?
- I think I created a monster.

1859
01:43:33,531 --> 01:43:35,197
Oh, you think so?

1860
01:43:35,198 --> 01:43:38,447
He got some pretty dope concepts
in that pile of madness. He's just...

1861
01:43:38,448 --> 01:43:40,489
Execution is all over the place.

1862
01:43:40,490 --> 01:43:42,655
Mm, look at you bein' a proud mama!

1863
01:43:42,656 --> 01:43:44,864
I'm just saying, okay?

1864
01:43:44,865 --> 01:43:46,031
He's cool.

1865
01:43:46,740 --> 01:43:49,406
Neurotic and annoying, but...

1866
01:43:50,115 --> 01:43:51,115
but cool.

1867
01:43:51,740 --> 01:43:53,947
[smacks lips] He's kinda like you!

1868
01:43:53,948 --> 01:43:56,447
Ain't no way
you disrespectin' me like that.

1869
01:43:56,448 --> 01:44:00,364
- Aw.
- He could never, okay? He wishes.

1870
01:44:00,365 --> 01:44:01,823
[chuckling]

1871
01:44:04,240 --> 01:44:05,447
Do you... [laughs]

1872
01:44:05,448 --> 01:44:11,155
Do you remember when Gary caught me
and Shawn Porter canoodling in his shop?

1873
01:44:11,156 --> 01:44:13,322
He was so disappointed.

1874
01:44:13,323 --> 01:44:16,323
Yeah, that was his sacred space,
and then here you and--

1875
01:44:17,740 --> 01:44:23,155
Here Nat and pimply, hormonal Shawn
come defiling it.

1876
01:44:23,156 --> 01:44:25,072
Oh, wow, "defiling."

1877
01:44:25,073 --> 01:44:26,989
- Yes.
- Well, you could stay mad

1878
01:44:26,990 --> 01:44:28,322
over at the kiddie table.

1879
01:44:28,323 --> 01:44:33,740
And, um, I caught that "you" slip,
by the way.

1880
01:44:34,365 --> 01:44:36,281
[Natalie sighs]

1881
01:44:37,365 --> 01:44:39,448
You know, I remember a lot of things.

1882
01:44:40,573 --> 01:44:43,656
Um, there are gaps, which suck, but...

1883
01:44:44,281 --> 01:44:48,114
I guess there'd be gaps
if I were alive, so...

1884
01:44:48,115 --> 01:44:50,031
[♪ wistful music playing]

1885
01:44:54,531 --> 01:44:56,656
I wonder what Gary
would think of the suit.

1886
01:44:58,823 --> 01:45:00,198
You know, seeing me fly it.

1887
01:45:01,906 --> 01:45:03,780
He'd probably trip.

1888
01:45:03,781 --> 01:45:05,239
- Yeah.
- [chuckling]

1889
01:45:05,240 --> 01:45:09,447
Especially if he knew
what you were doing with it.

1890
01:45:09,448 --> 01:45:10,739
All right, not this again.

1891
01:45:10,740 --> 01:45:13,280
Talk about defiling sacred spaces.

1892
01:45:13,281 --> 01:45:15,572
Meanwhile, you out here
bein' a whole-ass criminal.

1893
01:45:15,573 --> 01:45:18,656
- Man, I told you, I got it under control.
- For now.

1894
01:45:21,115 --> 01:45:22,364
[scoffs]

1895
01:45:22,365 --> 01:45:23,448
Okay.

1896
01:45:24,865 --> 01:45:25,990
Let's get out of here.

1897
01:45:26,865 --> 01:45:29,573
I have one last place I wanna go.

1898
01:45:34,365 --> 01:45:36,240
[Riri] This is where our day ends?

1899
01:45:36,823 --> 01:45:38,155
[Natalie] Mm-hmm.

1900
01:45:38,156 --> 01:45:39,698
You still got the key, right?

1901
01:45:41,365 --> 01:45:45,114
Oh, man, I haven't been here
since... [sighs]

1902
01:45:45,115 --> 01:45:48,030
well, you know, but it might help you
get some perspective.

1903
01:45:48,031 --> 01:45:49,280
I never lost perspective.

1904
01:45:49,281 --> 01:45:53,530
I figure, after this, it's one...
two more jobs, and then I am out.

1905
01:45:53,531 --> 01:45:55,448
And you think Parker will let you go?

1906
01:45:56,281 --> 01:45:57,781
He's not Al Capone.

1907
01:45:59,740 --> 01:46:01,655
Well, all right, then.

1908
01:46:01,656 --> 01:46:03,655
- [lock opening]
- All right, open the doors.

1909
01:46:03,656 --> 01:46:05,490
Let's see what we workin' with.

1910
01:46:12,948 --> 01:46:15,573
Oh, my God.

1911
01:46:16,365 --> 01:46:18,865
This is crazy!

1912
01:46:20,781 --> 01:46:23,948
Does it still smell the same, Ri?

1913
01:46:24,656 --> 01:46:26,905
- [Natalie laughs]
- [high-pitched ringing]

1914
01:46:26,906 --> 01:46:29,280
- Ew, cobwebs.
- [♪ melancholy music playing]

1915
01:46:29,281 --> 01:46:31,448
Oh, snap!

1916
01:46:33,406 --> 01:46:34,698
It's my gum!

1917
01:46:37,115 --> 01:46:38,239
Ri.

1918
01:46:38,240 --> 01:46:39,780
[tires screeching]

1919
01:46:39,781 --> 01:46:44,030
Come, come see
if this still has flav-- fla-- flav--

1920
01:46:44,031 --> 01:46:45,239
[Natalie and Gary] Riri.

1921
01:46:45,240 --> 01:46:46,364
[Gary] Riri?

1922
01:46:46,365 --> 01:46:48,239
- [Natalie] Riri?
- [Gary] Girls, run!

1923
01:46:48,240 --> 01:46:49,573
[gunshots]

1924
01:46:50,740 --> 01:46:52,405
Natalie!

1925
01:46:52,406 --> 01:46:54,448
[gunshots]

1926
01:46:55,115 --> 01:46:56,448
Natalie?

1927
01:46:57,115 --> 01:46:58,948
- [gunshots]
- [tires screeching]

1928
01:47:01,490 --> 01:47:06,740
<i>N-Natalie? Da-Dad? Natalie!</i>

1929
01:47:07,615 --> 01:47:08,822
[Riri sobs]

1930
01:47:08,823 --> 01:47:10,697
[muffled] Oh. Ri?

1931
01:47:10,698 --> 01:47:12,530
- [high-pitched ringing continues]
- Oh, wait, um...

1932
01:47:12,531 --> 01:47:14,072
[shuddering]

1933
01:47:14,073 --> 01:47:16,822
Uh, okay, okay,
if you can hear, I think we're--

1934
01:47:16,823 --> 01:47:19,281
I think you're havin' a panic attack.

1935
01:47:19,948 --> 01:47:22,989
But it's okay. It's okay!
Hey, all you have to do is breathe.

1936
01:47:22,990 --> 01:47:24,405
Okay? In and out.

1937
01:47:24,406 --> 01:47:25,614
Can you do that with me?

1938
01:47:25,615 --> 01:47:26,697
In.

1939
01:47:26,698 --> 01:47:28,572
[both breathing deeply]

1940
01:47:28,573 --> 01:47:30,240
Out. Okay. In.

1941
01:47:31,115 --> 01:47:32,990
Slower. Out.

1942
01:47:34,115 --> 01:47:37,864
Good, again. In. Out.

1943
01:47:37,865 --> 01:47:39,573
[high-pitched ringing ceases]

1944
01:47:42,656 --> 01:47:45,156
Okay. That was good. You all right?

1945
01:47:47,323 --> 01:47:48,906
How do you know how to do that?

1946
01:47:49,573 --> 01:47:50,615
How do I know how to do what?

1947
01:47:51,740 --> 01:47:52,823
My Nat...

1948
01:47:54,323 --> 01:47:57,406
was the only one who knew
how to calm me down when I spiraled.

1949
01:48:00,490 --> 01:48:01,489
Uh...

1950
01:48:01,490 --> 01:48:03,198
[♪ pensive music playing]

1951
01:48:05,740 --> 01:48:06,740
Thank you.

1952
01:48:08,656 --> 01:48:10,031
Anytime.

1953
01:48:13,698 --> 01:48:15,614
[Ronnie] She just came home from college.

1954
01:48:15,615 --> 01:48:17,572
Can you tell me what this is about?

1955
01:48:17,573 --> 01:48:19,365
[indistinct police radio chatter]

1956
01:48:20,948 --> 01:48:23,948
Riri, these officers are here for you.

1957
01:48:25,240 --> 01:48:31,322
Ms. Williams, I'd like to ask you
about your relationship to Stuart Clarke.

1958
01:48:31,323 --> 01:48:33,989
Expert computer tech. Street name Rampage,

1959
01:48:33,990 --> 01:48:35,406
- if that helps.
- Rampage?

1960
01:48:36,698 --> 01:48:37,822
Did he say he knew me?

1961
01:48:37,823 --> 01:48:39,406
He was found dead this morning.

1962
01:48:40,031 --> 01:48:42,155
We're investigating it
as a potential homicide.

1963
01:48:42,156 --> 01:48:44,322
You think Riri
has something to do with this?

1964
01:48:44,323 --> 01:48:47,406
Our phone logs indicate
she is the last person to have called him.

1965
01:48:49,490 --> 01:48:50,490
I...

1966
01:48:51,115 --> 01:48:53,406
I met Stuart on a job interview.

1967
01:48:54,115 --> 01:48:55,573
We exchanged numbers.

1968
01:48:56,406 --> 01:48:59,156
I tried reaching out once,
but he never answered.

1969
01:49:00,365 --> 01:49:03,780
[officer] Well, if anything else comes
to mind, we'd appreciate a call.

1970
01:49:03,781 --> 01:49:05,698
[♪ tense music playing]

1971
01:49:09,406 --> 01:49:10,490
Riri, are you okay?

1972
01:49:11,906 --> 01:49:12,948
Yeah, I'm good.

1973
01:49:16,365 --> 01:49:17,364
[door shuts]

1974
01:49:17,365 --> 01:49:18,947
Stuart got murked.

1975
01:49:18,948 --> 01:49:22,364
Yo, they could've murked me.
I knew somethin' was off! Shit!

1976
01:49:22,365 --> 01:49:23,655
Shit, shit, shit!

1977
01:49:23,656 --> 01:49:26,155
[whimpers] Riri, what were you thinking?

1978
01:49:26,156 --> 01:49:31,447
I know I haven't exactly been Team Parker,
but we don't know that.

1979
01:49:31,448 --> 01:49:34,615
They know everything about me. About Ma.

1980
01:49:35,490 --> 01:49:36,490
Xa.

1981
01:49:38,281 --> 01:49:39,905
- I need to get ahead of this.
- Yeah.

1982
01:49:39,906 --> 01:49:41,030
- Ahead of him.
- Yeah.

1983
01:49:41,031 --> 01:49:43,823
- I need that hood.
- No! Are you crazy?

1984
01:49:44,448 --> 01:49:46,614
Do you know what you're up against?

1985
01:49:46,615 --> 01:49:49,572
This magical dude
can bend bullets to his will.

1986
01:49:49,573 --> 01:49:53,405
- If he can do that, what else can he do?
- First, he's not magic.

1987
01:49:53,406 --> 01:49:56,947
He's just a regular dude
in a magical outfit. He said so himself.

1988
01:49:56,948 --> 01:49:59,239
- He's nothing without that hood.
- Hmm.

1989
01:49:59,240 --> 01:50:01,655
Second, that's exactly why
we need to get that hood.

1990
01:50:01,656 --> 01:50:04,614
- So we can know what we're up against.
- And how exactly do we

1991
01:50:04,615 --> 01:50:06,656
analyze it without ending up like Rampage?

1992
01:50:07,656 --> 01:50:09,156
That part...

1993
01:50:09,698 --> 01:50:11,073
[cell phone vibrating]

1994
01:50:12,865 --> 01:50:14,323
- Was that you?
- Was that you?

1995
01:50:20,615 --> 01:50:21,656
Shit!

1996
01:50:23,740 --> 01:50:25,489
The Heirlum heist.

1997
01:50:25,490 --> 01:50:27,697
Parker will be so distracted
with Hunter Mason,

1998
01:50:27,698 --> 01:50:30,114
he won't even notice if I take
just a little piece of his hood.

1999
01:50:30,115 --> 01:50:32,114
- Mm.
- Just gotta figure out how to get in.

2000
01:50:32,115 --> 01:50:33,405
Yeah, I could figure that out.

2001
01:50:33,406 --> 01:50:35,280
We still have the Heirlum design plan.

2002
01:50:35,281 --> 01:50:38,530
Nah. Slug said
Heirlum's security is tight.

2003
01:50:38,531 --> 01:50:42,198
I can't even bring my suit inside,
but without it, I'm completely naked.

2004
01:50:43,073 --> 01:50:44,073
Joe.

2005
01:50:45,656 --> 01:50:49,280
Okay, I'm not even gonna get into
how awkward that transition was.

2006
01:50:49,281 --> 01:50:50,364
What? Tsk.

2007
01:50:50,365 --> 01:50:53,947
Ew, nah. Joe has a prototype
for a bio-mesh skin material.

2008
01:50:53,948 --> 01:50:55,822
It can cover bionic enhancements.

2009
01:50:55,823 --> 01:50:58,655
Might need a bit of tinkering,
but I think it could be just what I need.

2010
01:50:58,656 --> 01:51:00,322
Well, how convenient.

2011
01:51:00,323 --> 01:51:01,406
Yeah.

2012
01:51:02,031 --> 01:51:04,698
Joe's prototype is about to get
a Riri Williams upgrade.

2013
01:51:06,365 --> 01:51:07,365
[laughs]

2014
01:51:11,365 --> 01:51:13,155
[reporter on TV]
<i>Chicago crime has found its way</i>

2015
01:51:13,156 --> 01:51:16,905
{\an8}<i>into the city's growing tech industry
as of late.</i>

2016
01:51:16,906 --> 01:51:21,239
<i>The Chicago PD has been responding to
a string of highly sophisticated attacks</i>

2017
01:51:21,240 --> 01:51:22,447
<i>on some of the most...</i>

2018
01:51:22,448 --> 01:51:25,406
Heyo. Hot dang, it's hot in here, bro.

2019
01:51:26,198 --> 01:51:28,948
Hottest day in October, man,
you really need the heat on?

2020
01:51:32,573 --> 01:51:33,822
[John exhales heavily]

2021
01:51:33,823 --> 01:51:35,364
[breathing deeply]

2022
01:51:35,365 --> 01:51:41,031
Yo, don't yoga people listen to gongs,
not news about their own illegal activity?

2023
01:51:41,948 --> 01:51:44,614
This couldn't wait
until after I finished the vinyasa?

2024
01:51:44,615 --> 01:51:45,698
Nope.

2025
01:51:47,573 --> 01:51:48,740
- [sighs quietly]
- Look, man.

2026
01:51:50,490 --> 01:51:52,073
We gotta cancel this Heirlum gig.

2027
01:51:53,240 --> 01:51:56,197
Nah, for real, I can't find anything
on Hunter Mason, man.

2028
01:51:56,198 --> 01:51:58,197
No vices, no weaknesses.

2029
01:51:58,198 --> 01:51:59,990
Hell, no social media.

2030
01:52:00,740 --> 01:52:02,155
That's weird.

2031
01:52:02,156 --> 01:52:06,572
Look. All I got is his ex-girlfriend
claiming that the prince of sustainability

2032
01:52:06,573 --> 01:52:08,155
takes 30-minute showers.

2033
01:52:08,156 --> 01:52:11,405
We got no idea what we're walkin' into.
We should just cancel this thing.

2034
01:52:11,406 --> 01:52:14,114
- We run that risk with every hit, John.
- Yeah, but this is different--

2035
01:52:14,115 --> 01:52:16,280
We'll find the crack. We always do.

2036
01:52:16,281 --> 01:52:17,864
If we can't find a pressure point,

2037
01:52:17,865 --> 01:52:20,614
then maybe we'll just have Riri hit 'em
with the one-two, you know?

2038
01:52:20,615 --> 01:52:22,614
Punch a hole in the building
and make one. [chuckles]

2039
01:52:22,615 --> 01:52:23,698
This funny?

2040
01:52:25,073 --> 01:52:26,780
I need your head in the game.

2041
01:52:26,781 --> 01:52:28,947
Says the guy who's tryin'
to derail the whole mission

2042
01:52:28,948 --> 01:52:30,655
because he's scared
of a glorified gardener.

2043
01:52:30,656 --> 01:52:32,573
We got no leverage, man.

2044
01:52:33,115 --> 01:52:36,322
Riri's suit ain't a catch-all.
It's making you too confident.

2045
01:52:36,323 --> 01:52:38,780
You think the crew
would be down to go into this half-ass

2046
01:52:38,781 --> 01:52:41,447
just because you're blinded
by your personal vendetta?

2047
01:52:41,448 --> 01:52:42,740
Think, Parker.

2048
01:52:44,240 --> 01:52:46,739
- Maybe you're right.
- [reporter] <i>The founder and CEO</i>

2049
01:52:46,740 --> 01:52:48,364
<i>- gave a public statement...</i>
- All right then.

2050
01:52:48,365 --> 01:52:51,905
<i>...regarding the crime saying,
"These are simply petty thieves</i>

2051
01:52:51,906 --> 01:52:54,447
<i>"trying to make a buck using cheap tricks.</i>

2052
01:52:54,448 --> 01:52:57,323
<i>"They will be caught
and brought to justice swiftly."</i>

2053
01:52:58,406 --> 01:52:59,572
<i>Local activist groups...</i>

2054
01:52:59,573 --> 01:53:00,656
No.

2055
01:53:01,406 --> 01:53:02,906
We hit the target as planned.

2056
01:53:04,656 --> 01:53:05,656
Oh.

2057
01:53:06,698 --> 01:53:08,280
Oh, okay.

2058
01:53:08,281 --> 01:53:09,573
That's how it is, huh?

2059
01:53:10,281 --> 01:53:11,281
No discussion?

2060
01:53:12,573 --> 01:53:15,572
Man, we used to do this together.
Maybe you don't need my help no more.

2061
01:53:15,573 --> 01:53:19,281
It's just that I see
the bigger picture, John.

2062
01:53:22,865 --> 01:53:23,865
You gotta trust me.

2063
01:53:25,573 --> 01:53:26,656
I do trust you, man.

2064
01:53:29,698 --> 01:53:31,948
You say we hit Heirlum,
then I got your back.

2065
01:53:32,781 --> 01:53:34,198
[doorbell rings]

2066
01:53:35,323 --> 01:53:38,114
- Hey, Joe. Sorry I'm late-- Oh.
- [Joe] Riri! Thank goodness.

2067
01:53:38,115 --> 01:53:39,198
[door closes]

2068
01:53:39,740 --> 01:53:41,780
Uh, what happened to this place?

2069
01:53:41,781 --> 01:53:45,030
[Joe] I was trying to create a neurolink
between myself and a computer chip

2070
01:53:45,031 --> 01:53:47,197
- to test the potential for prosthetic--
- Dude!

2071
01:53:47,198 --> 01:53:48,530
[Joe] Mm, whoopsie.

2072
01:53:48,531 --> 01:53:50,655
- What did you do?
- I thought the install

2073
01:53:50,656 --> 01:53:52,739
would be simple enough,
I must've shorted something.

2074
01:53:52,740 --> 01:53:54,572
Can you just... Would you help?

2075
01:53:54,573 --> 01:53:56,240
Would you mind?

2076
01:53:57,490 --> 01:54:01,240
I need some tweezers, alcohol, and...

2077
01:54:02,573 --> 01:54:04,739
You happen to have any of that
bio-mesh skin you designed?

2078
01:54:04,740 --> 01:54:07,406
Uh, there's some on the counter.

2079
01:54:08,198 --> 01:54:09,781
- [squelches]
- [inhales sharply]

2080
01:54:10,781 --> 01:54:13,448
Just kidding. I put local anesthetic.

2081
01:54:14,490 --> 01:54:18,031
So, uh, was checking out
your schematics and...

2082
01:54:18,906 --> 01:54:20,656
I got some ideas for that bio-mesh.

2083
01:54:21,281 --> 01:54:23,489
- Yeah?
- We could upgrade it

2084
01:54:23,490 --> 01:54:25,990
with some enhanced silicone gridding
for cloaking.

2085
01:54:30,031 --> 01:54:32,573
Cloaking from X-rays, metal detectors...

2086
01:54:34,031 --> 01:54:37,114
Couldn't that be used to violate
all kinds of concealed weapon laws?

2087
01:54:37,115 --> 01:54:38,865
Yes, narc.

2088
01:54:39,615 --> 01:54:42,572
But, it'd take me 10 minutes, tops.

2089
01:54:42,573 --> 01:54:44,489
You already have all the parts I'd need.

2090
01:54:44,490 --> 01:54:46,155
Thing is, I'd just need to borrow it.

2091
01:54:46,156 --> 01:54:48,489
I knew it. No, no, no, no.

2092
01:54:48,490 --> 01:54:51,780
Just to test it. I'll be careful.

2093
01:54:51,781 --> 01:54:54,739
This is all black market tech
that I've heavily modified

2094
01:54:54,740 --> 01:54:56,239
and could be traced back to me.

2095
01:54:56,240 --> 01:54:58,864
You can't use it to make something
even more illegal.

2096
01:54:58,865 --> 01:55:00,155
But, Joe,

2097
01:55:00,156 --> 01:55:02,490
- we can make something legit.
- [sighs]

2098
01:55:04,156 --> 01:55:05,656
Think about the possibilities.

2099
01:55:07,115 --> 01:55:08,655
Yo, where's your peroxide?

2100
01:55:08,656 --> 01:55:10,490
[Joe] The left of the sink.

2101
01:55:11,240 --> 01:55:12,656
[rattling]

2102
01:55:15,031 --> 01:55:17,655
And what is this? Wheat flour?

2103
01:55:17,656 --> 01:55:19,740
No. Riri, please put Dad back.

2104
01:55:20,656 --> 01:55:22,239
Dad?

2105
01:55:22,240 --> 01:55:24,697
What the hell, Joe? Why is he in a baggie?

2106
01:55:24,698 --> 01:55:26,240
[sighs] We weren't close.

2107
01:55:27,073 --> 01:55:29,031
Now he's everywhere. Great.

2108
01:55:29,865 --> 01:55:31,240
[whirring]

2109
01:55:37,573 --> 01:55:38,989
[Riri] Obadiah?

2110
01:55:38,990 --> 01:55:40,072
Let's see.

2111
01:55:40,073 --> 01:55:44,572
A rich techie
whose dad's name was Obadiah S!

2112
01:55:44,573 --> 01:55:45,947
Obadiah's a common name.

2113
01:55:45,948 --> 01:55:48,198
It absolutely is not. Anywhere.

2114
01:55:52,073 --> 01:55:53,865
Who are you really, Joe?

2115
01:55:56,323 --> 01:55:58,156
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

2116
01:55:58,781 --> 01:55:59,781
[sighs]

2117
01:56:01,490 --> 01:56:02,740
I'm Ezekiel...

2118
01:56:03,990 --> 01:56:04,990
Stane.

2119
01:56:05,865 --> 01:56:06,865
As in...

2120
01:56:07,490 --> 01:56:08,740
Obadiah Stane?

2121
01:56:09,406 --> 01:56:12,072
- COO of Stark Industries?
- [keypad tapping]

2122
01:56:12,073 --> 01:56:15,155
BFF to Howard Stark? Mentor to Tony Stark,

2123
01:56:15,156 --> 01:56:18,156
died in that horrible plane crash,
that Stane?

2124
01:56:22,406 --> 01:56:23,656
The plane story's a lie.

2125
01:56:25,198 --> 01:56:26,405
[sighs]

2126
01:56:26,406 --> 01:56:27,531
The truth is...

2127
01:56:28,406 --> 01:56:31,823
Dad died trying to kill Tony Stark.

2128
01:56:34,448 --> 01:56:38,948
And I've spent my whole life
trying to be nothing like him.

2129
01:56:39,823 --> 01:56:41,573
He went full supervillain,

2130
01:56:42,281 --> 01:56:43,697
in a suit a lot like yours.

2131
01:56:43,698 --> 01:56:47,489
Then you come knocking on my door,
with that same iron energy.

2132
01:56:47,490 --> 01:56:49,239
It's a non-starter, Riri.

2133
01:56:49,240 --> 01:56:51,531
I cannot be a party
to whatever you're up to.

2134
01:56:54,281 --> 01:56:56,115
[♪ pensive music playing]

2135
01:57:06,656 --> 01:57:07,865
I didn't know.

2136
01:57:10,323 --> 01:57:11,865
Sorry I asked.

2137
01:57:26,615 --> 01:57:27,823
[sighs]

2138
01:57:39,406 --> 01:57:40,573
You're good at this.

2139
01:57:41,531 --> 01:57:42,906
Could've been a doctor.

2140
01:57:43,948 --> 01:57:45,573
Anything really.

2141
01:57:47,115 --> 01:57:48,323
Why the Iron suit?

2142
01:57:50,281 --> 01:57:51,280
'Cause I could.

2143
01:57:51,281 --> 01:57:52,822
You said that before.

2144
01:57:52,823 --> 01:57:54,239
Why'd you really build it?

2145
01:57:54,240 --> 01:57:55,448
Were you a fan?

2146
01:58:03,323 --> 01:58:04,823
The real fan was Gary,

2147
01:58:06,240 --> 01:58:07,573
my stepdad.

2148
01:58:09,573 --> 01:58:12,615
[chuckles] He used to talk about Stark
all the time.

2149
01:58:13,490 --> 01:58:16,322
He was a mechanic,
but he had an aviator's brain.

2150
01:58:16,323 --> 01:58:20,280
He used to say, "The best way
to know a thing is to take it apart,

2151
01:58:20,281 --> 01:58:21,823
"put it back together.

2152
01:58:22,865 --> 01:58:24,990
"Then you can see
what it's really made of."

2153
01:58:27,740 --> 01:58:30,447
He fixed broken things all the time

2154
01:58:30,448 --> 01:58:32,031
because he could.

2155
01:58:33,115 --> 01:58:34,697
A broken car.

2156
01:58:34,698 --> 01:58:37,406
A broken home with a broken-hearted widow.

2157
01:58:40,406 --> 01:58:41,656
Even broken me.

2158
01:58:43,740 --> 01:58:45,323
That's the kinda hero he was.

2159
01:58:46,990 --> 01:58:48,531
Was?

2160
01:58:51,156 --> 01:58:52,240
Drive-by.

2161
01:58:54,406 --> 01:58:55,573
I'm sorry.

2162
01:58:57,865 --> 01:58:59,322
At least you had a good example.

2163
01:58:59,323 --> 01:59:00,864
I had an Iron Monger.

2164
01:59:00,865 --> 01:59:01,948
[chuckles lightly]

2165
01:59:02,865 --> 01:59:04,031
You're lucky...

2166
01:59:05,156 --> 01:59:07,115
to have had a Gary.

2167
01:59:11,698 --> 01:59:12,865
Nobody asked,

2168
01:59:14,240 --> 01:59:16,740
but I'd say you're not your dad
unless you choose to be.

2169
01:59:17,448 --> 01:59:18,448
You're your own you.

2170
01:59:20,698 --> 01:59:22,823
And that guy's pretty powerful on his own.

2171
01:59:27,031 --> 01:59:28,156
Okay,

2172
01:59:28,865 --> 01:59:29,948
done.

2173
01:59:36,240 --> 01:59:37,406
We're friends, right?

2174
01:59:46,823 --> 01:59:48,323
[objects rattle, lid closes]

2175
01:59:50,031 --> 01:59:51,448
Implement your upgrades.

2176
01:59:52,115 --> 01:59:53,656
But promise me,

2177
01:59:54,740 --> 01:59:56,323
this won't blow back on me.

2178
01:59:59,656 --> 02:00:00,740
I swear...

2179
02:00:01,781 --> 02:00:02,781
Zeke.

2180
02:00:04,823 --> 02:00:06,740
You should take Gary's advice.

2181
02:00:08,115 --> 02:00:11,073
Break yourself down,
see what you're made of.

2182
02:00:12,031 --> 02:00:13,948
Might be a hero in there after all.

2183
02:00:18,323 --> 02:00:19,698
You got this in, like, a

2184
02:00:20,573 --> 02:00:22,030
Cocoa Sunrise?

2185
02:00:22,031 --> 02:00:23,948
[♪ dramatic music playing]

2186
02:00:26,865 --> 02:00:28,115
[Slug] All right.

2187
02:00:28,906 --> 02:00:30,823
Last metal check
before we head to Heirlum.

2188
02:00:31,490 --> 02:00:33,614
The sensors in these greenhouses
are no joke.

2189
02:00:33,615 --> 02:00:34,864
[kisses] You'll be okay.

2190
02:00:34,865 --> 02:00:37,197
[Slug] If we're gonna have a shot
at holding Heirlum hostage,

2191
02:00:37,198 --> 02:00:39,573
everyone needs to stick to their jobs.
Got it?

2192
02:00:50,323 --> 02:00:52,947
[Natalie] Are you sure
that bio-mesh in white boy nude

2193
02:00:52,948 --> 02:00:55,447
is gonna make it past
Heirlum's metal detectors?

2194
02:00:55,448 --> 02:01:00,114
I'm not, but it's the only piece of tech
I have to cloak my laser cutters,

2195
02:01:00,115 --> 02:01:04,947
so, let's get that hood piece, get our
leverage, and get the hell out of dodge.

2196
02:01:04,948 --> 02:01:06,030
[Slug] <i>Ri, you there?</i>

2197
02:01:06,031 --> 02:01:07,655
[Riri] Yeah, I'm here.

2198
02:01:07,656 --> 02:01:09,114
[Slug] <i>Tell us what you see.</i>

2199
02:01:09,115 --> 02:01:12,948
I see five greenhouses
surrounding a central atrium.

2200
02:01:16,323 --> 02:01:17,865
Security seems light out here.

2201
02:01:18,406 --> 02:01:23,115
[Slug] <i>My turn.
Cameras down in three, two, one.</i>

2202
02:01:25,656 --> 02:01:27,031
You're clear.

2203
02:01:28,490 --> 02:01:30,656
[The Hood] <i>Good job, Riri.
Head to the watch tower.</i>

2204
02:01:37,781 --> 02:01:39,781
[electricity thudding and crackling]

2205
02:01:40,365 --> 02:01:41,364
[Riri] Lights out.

2206
02:01:41,365 --> 02:01:43,656
<i>Blood sibs, do your thing.</i>

2207
02:01:44,365 --> 02:01:47,697
[guard] <i>Hey, Mike, I'm seeing some lights
out by you. Can you check it out?</i>

2208
02:01:47,698 --> 02:01:48,864
[Roz] Hello.

2209
02:01:48,865 --> 02:01:50,031
[grunting]

2210
02:01:53,115 --> 02:01:56,239
[Riri chuckles] Whoo.
Nice clothesline, Roz.

2211
02:01:56,240 --> 02:01:58,531
Okay, storm the castle.

2212
02:02:00,948 --> 02:02:02,405
[both grunting]

2213
02:02:02,406 --> 02:02:04,947
- [guard grunting]
- [Jeri] <i>Jeri and Slug are in position.</i>

2214
02:02:04,948 --> 02:02:07,239
[John] <i>Copy. Clown? Roz?</i>

2215
02:02:07,240 --> 02:02:08,905
Yeah, we're set.

2216
02:02:08,906 --> 02:02:10,990
Stay by the watch tower, Riri.
I'm comin' to you.

2217
02:02:11,823 --> 02:02:12,906
[Riri] <i>Copy that.</i>

2218
02:02:14,365 --> 02:02:15,490
I'm staying put.

2219
02:02:16,781 --> 02:02:18,781
[♪ suspenseful music playing]

2220
02:02:24,531 --> 02:02:25,864
[sighs]

2221
02:02:25,865 --> 02:02:28,031
[Natalie] Relax. You got this, Ri.

2222
02:03:02,823 --> 02:03:05,490
<i>All right, my little mermaid.</i>

2223
02:03:06,406 --> 02:03:09,656
<i>Hunter's office is right
in the center of the structure.</i>

2224
02:03:10,240 --> 02:03:13,240
<i>Follow the pipe
and when you get to the fork, go up.</i>

2225
02:03:15,115 --> 02:03:16,573
[gasping]

2226
02:03:17,240 --> 02:03:18,281
I'm climbin' up.

2227
02:03:19,281 --> 02:03:21,822
[Natalie] <i>Okay. Now, chances are,
you're gonna enter</i>

2228
02:03:21,823 --> 02:03:23,572
directly underneath Hunter and Parker,

2229
02:03:23,573 --> 02:03:26,240
so please be careful.

2230
02:03:27,865 --> 02:03:29,906
Yo, Iron Head, focus.

2231
02:03:31,615 --> 02:03:33,947
Ri, let me punch this dude
right in the throat.

2232
02:03:33,948 --> 02:03:36,989
- [Riri] <i>Nat, don't do anything.</i>
- [John] <i>Me and Riri are set.</i>

2233
02:03:36,990 --> 02:03:39,240
- [Clown] <i>Where's the boss?</i>
- [John] <i>Movin' in on Hunter Mason.</i>

2234
02:03:40,490 --> 02:03:42,780
[♪ soothing music playing on speakers]

2235
02:03:42,781 --> 02:03:43,948
[Hunter] Drink up.

2236
02:03:55,031 --> 02:03:56,865
[heavy breathing]

2237
02:03:58,698 --> 02:03:59,698
I know you're there.

2238
02:04:02,948 --> 02:04:04,031
Apologies.

2239
02:04:04,865 --> 02:04:06,698
I let myself in.

2240
02:04:07,365 --> 02:04:09,280
A little gauche, I know.

2241
02:04:09,281 --> 02:04:12,864
[Hunter] There have been whispers
about a flamboyant young upstart

2242
02:04:12,865 --> 02:04:14,739
- pruning the competition.
- [cover scrapes]

2243
02:04:14,740 --> 02:04:16,947
I was wondering when you'd pay me a visit.

2244
02:04:16,948 --> 02:04:18,823
[panting]

2245
02:04:20,865 --> 02:04:22,115
[The Hood] You're writin' a check?

2246
02:04:23,115 --> 02:04:26,155
[Hunter] Well, that's what
this is all about, isn't it? Money?

2247
02:04:26,156 --> 02:04:27,823
[trilling]

2248
02:04:28,990 --> 02:04:31,655
[whispering] Nat, it worked. I'm in.

2249
02:04:31,656 --> 02:04:33,197
[Natalie] <i>Okay, all right.</i>

2250
02:04:33,198 --> 02:04:35,739
<i>Now, get the hood,
make like a tree and leaf.</i>

2251
02:04:35,740 --> 02:04:37,905
[Natalie snickering]

2252
02:04:37,906 --> 02:04:39,864
<i>- You see what I did there?</i>
- Nat!

2253
02:04:39,865 --> 02:04:42,072
You don't have anything better
to do right now?

2254
02:04:42,073 --> 02:04:44,947
[Natalie] <i>Okay, I'm sorry,
I'm sorry. You're doing great, just fine.</i>

2255
02:04:44,948 --> 02:04:48,155
This should be more than enough
to keep you and your friends happy.

2256
02:04:48,156 --> 02:04:50,322
[The Hood] Nope. This isn't about money.

2257
02:04:50,323 --> 02:04:51,572
[Hunter] Come on, man.

2258
02:04:51,573 --> 02:04:54,447
Of course it is.
I can see through this little act.

2259
02:04:54,448 --> 02:04:58,530
[The Hood chuckles] You can?
Well, why don't you tell me about myself.

2260
02:04:58,531 --> 02:05:00,530
[Hunter] It's not about the money,

2261
02:05:00,531 --> 02:05:02,115
it's about stealing the money.

2262
02:05:02,865 --> 02:05:05,031
Ri, you gotta be careful with that meter.

2263
02:05:07,573 --> 02:05:11,030
[Hunter] Yeah, losers like you get off on
taking somebody else's hard-earned cash,

2264
02:05:11,031 --> 02:05:12,656
especially somebodies like me.

2265
02:05:14,115 --> 02:05:15,114
[The Hood] Interesting read,

2266
02:05:15,115 --> 02:05:17,947
but I don't think you have a clear grasp
on the situation.

2267
02:05:17,948 --> 02:05:19,572
[Hunter] Take what I'm offering and run.

2268
02:05:19,573 --> 02:05:21,405
You can tell all your homies
you stole from me.

2269
02:05:21,406 --> 02:05:23,864
[The Hood] Whatever amount you wrote
on that check is a sneeze

2270
02:05:23,865 --> 02:05:25,823
compared to what we'll take. Here.

2271
02:05:28,365 --> 02:05:31,114
[chuckles] Are you joking
with this piece of paper?

2272
02:05:31,115 --> 02:05:32,614
Sign the contract.

2273
02:05:32,615 --> 02:05:33,865
No.

2274
02:05:36,948 --> 02:05:38,115
[The Hood] Suit yourself.

2275
02:05:39,448 --> 02:05:40,447
Do it, Slug.

2276
02:05:40,448 --> 02:05:41,948
[keyboard clacking]

2277
02:05:43,656 --> 02:05:44,948
[computer trilling]

2278
02:05:47,156 --> 02:05:50,614
[Slug] Fire detectors are now disabled.

2279
02:05:50,615 --> 02:05:52,405
[The Hood] <i>Clown, light it up.</i>

2280
02:05:52,406 --> 02:05:54,490
[♪ tense music playing]

2281
02:06:04,031 --> 02:06:05,156
[flame whooshes]

2282
02:06:06,865 --> 02:06:09,822
<i>We're gonna burn your greenhouses
one by one.</i>

2283
02:06:09,823 --> 02:06:13,239
You're actually willin' to risk
destroyin' everything you worked for?

2284
02:06:13,240 --> 02:06:15,655
[Hunter] Well, I'm not risking anything.
You are.

2285
02:06:15,656 --> 02:06:18,280
You think you can just waltz in here
and take this?

2286
02:06:18,281 --> 02:06:20,280
Take my company
that I built from nothing--

2287
02:06:20,281 --> 02:06:23,072
[The Hood] You don't know anything
about starting from nothing.

2288
02:06:23,073 --> 02:06:24,739
Me? My homies? We do.

2289
02:06:24,740 --> 02:06:26,947
Hunter, sign the contract.

2290
02:06:26,948 --> 02:06:29,030
- [Natalie] <i>Ri, the meter.</i>
- [Riri] I know.

2291
02:06:29,031 --> 02:06:30,989
[Natalie] <i>You don't wanna set off
any alarms.</i>

2292
02:06:30,990 --> 02:06:32,780
- [Riri] I'm almost there.
- [beeping]

2293
02:06:32,781 --> 02:06:33,865
Shit.

2294
02:06:35,823 --> 02:06:37,656
[continues beeping]

2295
02:06:38,573 --> 02:06:39,698
Roz!

2296
02:06:40,698 --> 02:06:42,489
Can you feel it?

2297
02:06:42,490 --> 02:06:45,489
- Can you feel the heat?
- [automated voice] <i>Doors sealed in three,</i>

2298
02:06:45,490 --> 02:06:46,572
<i>two, one.</i>

2299
02:06:46,573 --> 02:06:48,781
- [door shuts, lock clicks]
- [Clown laughing]

2300
02:06:49,990 --> 02:06:51,490
[alarm chimes]

2301
02:06:52,073 --> 02:06:54,406
- [electrical thud]
- [alarm chiming]

2302
02:06:55,156 --> 02:06:56,365
Slug, what was that?

2303
02:06:56,948 --> 02:06:58,239
[automated voice] <i>Doors sealed.</i>

2304
02:06:58,240 --> 02:07:01,822
<i>You are in violation
of Heirlum security clearance.</i>

2305
02:07:01,823 --> 02:07:02,905
Guys,

2306
02:07:02,906 --> 02:07:04,989
the bio-alert sensor's been triggered.

2307
02:07:04,990 --> 02:07:06,614
- Well, shut it down.
- I can't.

2308
02:07:06,615 --> 02:07:08,614
- They're what?
- [Roz] <i>Jeri, what's going on?</i>

2309
02:07:08,615 --> 02:07:11,447
- [John] Slug, say again.
- [Roz] <i>Guys, we gotta get out of here.</i>

2310
02:07:11,448 --> 02:07:13,739
- [alarms sounding]
- [John] <i>What the hell?</i>

2311
02:07:13,740 --> 02:07:17,364
Roz, Jeri, somebody talk to me.
Please tell me what's goin' on in there.

2312
02:07:17,365 --> 02:07:20,947
[automated voice] <i>Evacuate immediately
or be subject to suffocation.</i>

2313
02:07:20,948 --> 02:07:23,322
[Jeri] No, no, no.
Tell me that's not what I think it is.

2314
02:07:23,323 --> 02:07:24,531
[rapid beeping]

2315
02:07:25,990 --> 02:07:28,115
[John] <i>Everyone okay?
Come on, give me an update.</i>

2316
02:07:29,448 --> 02:07:30,489
[Slug] Hood?

2317
02:07:30,490 --> 02:07:32,198
We in danger, girl.

2318
02:07:32,865 --> 02:07:34,197
[gasps]

2319
02:07:34,198 --> 02:07:35,655
What the hell did you do?

2320
02:07:35,656 --> 02:07:36,947
I didn't do anything.

2321
02:07:36,948 --> 02:07:40,406
One of the geniuses in your crew triggered
the sensors and now they're all trapped.

2322
02:07:41,573 --> 02:07:44,614
Ri, I don't want you to panic
but they are losing their shit

2323
02:07:44,615 --> 02:07:46,947
<i>over at the greenhouses.
It's time for you to get outta there.</i>

2324
02:07:46,948 --> 02:07:48,447
[Hunter] They're as good as dead.

2325
02:07:48,448 --> 02:07:49,905
- You have no power here.
- [Jeri] <i>Help!</i>

2326
02:07:49,906 --> 02:07:52,322
- [John] <i>We need y'all's help.</i>
- [Hunter] Now, [clears throat]

2327
02:07:52,323 --> 02:07:54,031
take your pennies

2328
02:07:55,073 --> 02:07:56,531
and get out of my sight.

2329
02:07:59,115 --> 02:08:01,115
[voices whispering indistinctly]

2330
02:08:03,656 --> 02:08:06,031
I said get out of my sight.

2331
02:08:07,906 --> 02:08:09,323
[♪ tense music playing]

2332
02:08:11,698 --> 02:08:12,697
Okay.

2333
02:08:12,698 --> 02:08:14,281
[searing]

2334
02:08:15,865 --> 02:08:16,865
[inhales sharply]

2335
02:08:19,573 --> 02:08:20,615
[gasps]

2336
02:08:22,740 --> 02:08:23,739
[quietly] Shit.

2337
02:08:23,740 --> 02:08:25,698
[fabric rustling]

2338
02:08:27,740 --> 02:08:29,740
- [whooshing]
- [choking]

2339
02:08:31,281 --> 02:08:33,281
[♪ intense music playing]

2340
02:08:35,073 --> 02:08:36,405
[continues choking]

2341
02:08:36,406 --> 02:08:37,740
[bone cracks]

2342
02:08:40,031 --> 02:08:42,031
[♪ menacing music playing]

2343
02:08:57,698 --> 02:08:59,531
[♪ tense music playing]

2344
02:09:05,365 --> 02:09:07,822
- [Slug] <i>Greenhouses are attacking.</i>
- [John] <i>Okay, cut bait and run.</i>

2345
02:09:07,823 --> 02:09:09,197
[Roz] Clown, blast the doors open.

2346
02:09:09,198 --> 02:09:10,905
- Yo, I can't.
- [automated voice] <i>Doors sealed.</i>

2347
02:09:10,906 --> 02:09:14,322
<i>- You are in violation of Heirlum...</i>
- They're pumping in carbon dioxide.

2348
02:09:14,323 --> 02:09:15,697
And we're dead.

2349
02:09:15,698 --> 02:09:17,656
Lovely. [grunting]

2350
02:09:25,073 --> 02:09:27,072
[John] <i>Parker, did you get the signature?</i>

2351
02:09:27,073 --> 02:09:29,530
- Parker?
- [alarms continue sounding]

2352
02:09:29,531 --> 02:09:30,656
Parker!

2353
02:09:31,406 --> 02:09:32,405
Hey, Park--

2354
02:09:32,406 --> 02:09:34,948
Get to the greenhouses, okay?
I'm gonna go for Parker.

2355
02:09:45,406 --> 02:09:47,155
- [guard 1] Boss is down.
- [guard 2] What happened?

2356
02:09:47,156 --> 02:09:48,739
[guard 3] Anyone get eyes on the intruder?

2357
02:09:48,740 --> 02:09:50,114
[guard 4] Mr. Mason's not breathing.

2358
02:09:50,115 --> 02:09:51,280
Call an ambulance.

2359
02:09:51,281 --> 02:09:53,281
[guard 5 shushes] He could still be here.

2360
02:09:55,406 --> 02:09:56,823
[grunting]

2361
02:10:00,906 --> 02:10:01,906
[screams]

2362
